00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.750
So now, remember how
GPUs work, right?

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.830
So when we have something
like an aten:0 or an H100,

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.600
we're going to have
a whole bunch of SM,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.170
streaming multiprocessors.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.640
Within each SM is a
large number of units

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.540
that can do computation.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.470
We have Int32 ones or FP32 ones.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.030
And then each SM
is going to launch

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.420
a large number of threads.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.560
And we have the memory
hierarchy, which

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.600
is that we have DRAM, or global
memory, which is big and slow,

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.220
and then we've got caches
that are much faster.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.080
And in fact, you
see here there's

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.650
this thing called
the register file,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.950
this is very, very fast memory
that each thread can access.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.610
And we're going to be making
heavy use of these registers

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:09.290
as we write high performance
code for GPUs today.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.750
So the basic structure
for the execution model

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.780
is going to be we're going to
have a collection of thread

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.780
blocks, and a block is going
to be scheduled on a single SM.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.600
So this is the atomic
unit that we're

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.330
going to be thinking about,
especially when we write code

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:24.390
in things like Triton.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.940
And then within
each block, there's

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.540
going to be a whole
bunch of threads,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.957
and the threads
are actually going

00:03:28.957 --> 00:03:30.657
to be the ones doing
the computation.

00:03:30.657 --> 00:03:32.240
And so if you have
a vector and you're

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.365
going to be operating over
elements of that vector,

00:03:34.365 --> 00:03:36.573
you're going to write code
where each thread is going

00:03:36.573 --> 00:03:38.540
to go in and maybe operate
over a few elements

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.080
of that vector at once.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.340
And all the threads together
will process the vector

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.810
completely.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:48.110
So why do we have these
things called thread blocks?

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.780
Why not just have threads
and your big global context?

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.950
Well, thread blocks
can communicate

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.840
with each other, their shared
memory kind of within the SM

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.000
that's pretty fast.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.560
So when you need to do something
like matrix multiplication,

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.227
you're going to need
to pass information

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:03.255
from thread to thread.

00:04:03.255 --> 00:04:04.630
And within a thread
block, that's

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.000
very fast, across thread
blocks or across these groups,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.380
it's going to be very expensive.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.595
So any data that
you need, you're

00:04:11.595 --> 00:04:13.720
going to want to keep within
the same thread block,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.029
or within the same
tile, and that's going

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:17.950
to keep things very, very fast.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.300
And that's going to be
as fast as L1 cache,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.829
and that's a great place to be.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.670
And so you can use this to
synchronize across threads,

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.710
but you can't, for example,
synchronize across blocks.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.930
You can't really control
what's going to happen.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.030
And remember, the thing
that I mentioned last week,

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.370
there's this thing called waves.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.770
Waves aren't an inherent thing
that you normally think about,

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.020
but for performance, it
is an important component.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.480
So when we actually
run these things,

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.160
the threads are grouped
into consecutive blocks

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.580
of 32 threads.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:53.130
And that's a wave, and that gets
executed kind of all at once

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.330
in an SM.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.220
And so one thing that
we would like to do

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.637
is to make sure
all the waves have

00:04:57.637 --> 00:04:59.500
an equal amount of computation.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.388
We can't always do
that, but if we can,

00:05:02.388 --> 00:05:03.430
we would like to do that.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.763
So we want to make the number
of thread blocks, ideally.

00:05:05.763 --> 00:05:07.620
Divide the number of
SMs, and to make sure

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.410
that each wave has an
equal amount of work.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.410
So we're going to ideally have a
lot more thread blocks than SMs.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.410
And we're going to try
to make that happen as we

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.020
write high performance code.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:19.660
OK.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.582
And then the last
concept, and maybe

00:05:21.582 --> 00:05:23.040
amongst the most
important concepts

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.620
here, is arithmetic intensity.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.090
We would like to keep
arithmetic intensity high.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.120
We would like to have
more flops than we

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.610
have bytes of memory movement.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.370
And this is because, if
you remember the scaling

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.700
plot from last lecture,
our compute scaling

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.750
is much, much faster
than memory scaling.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:42.750
So a lot of the
time, computations

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.763
are going to end up
being memory bound,

00:05:44.763 --> 00:05:46.930
and we're not actually
getting all of the work done.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:51.210
So as a general rule, matrix
multiplication is compute bound.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.947
If we do it cleverly,
everything else

00:05:52.947 --> 00:05:54.530
is going to be memory
bound, and we're

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.050
going to try to cleverly
reduce the amount of things

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.300
that are memory bound
or how badly things

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.112
are memory bound, OK?

00:06:01.112 --> 00:06:04.812
So that's our very, very
brief review of GPUs.

00:06:04.812 --> 00:06:06.270
Hopefully, everyone
remembers this.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.980
You still have a fresh memory
of the execution model.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.340
Feel free to stop me and
ask questions if any of you

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.647
have lingering doubts or
questions about how this is all

00:06:17.647 --> 00:06:18.230
going to work.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:18.937
Yes?

00:06:18.937 --> 00:06:20.270
What was the function of a warp?

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:21.670
What was the
function of-- sorry?

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:22.170
A warp.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.600
A warp.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.150
A warp is essentially
a group of threads

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:27.480
that get executed together.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.390
And the reason
why warps exist is

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:33.060
that they reduce the amount of
control machinery that's needed,

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.900
because you're executing all
these threads at the same time,

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.580
you don't need a control
thing for each thread,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.440
you need them for blocks of 32.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.530
And so you see, for
example, there's

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:47.270
a lot more compute units than
there are warp schedulers.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.460
And so you're able to do
a lot more parallel work

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:50.520
without worrying about control.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.353
And this is one of the
trade offs with CPUs.

00:06:52.353 --> 00:06:57.650
CPUs, a lot more Silicon area
dedicated to control and branch

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.140
prediction and things like
this, whereas for GPUs,

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.060
much more emphasis
on computation

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.370
with simpler controls.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.606
OK.

00:07:05.606 --> 00:07:09.170
So now we're going to get
into newer content now.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:11.660
And I think if there's
one high level thing

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:15.300
to remember, it's, you want to
write high performance code,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.850
you should remember to
benchmark and profile your code.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.780
And that seems very obvious,
but I've seen a lot of things

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.877
where students or people go
in and they're like, well,

00:07:23.877 --> 00:07:25.460
I think this is the
bottleneck, so I'm

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.150
going to spend three
hours optimizing it.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.192
And it turns out it wasn't
the bottleneck at all.

00:07:29.192 --> 00:07:31.310
I'm sure it was fun,
but it was kind of time

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.910
that was misallocated.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.780
And so if you actually use
a high performance or a very

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.540
detailed profiler, you
can see exactly where

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:42.150
your bottlenecks are and exactly
what the machine is doing.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.810
And once you have that, you
can go and spend your efforts

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.703
in the most important parts
of your code execution.

00:07:47.703 --> 00:07:49.120
And so that's the
high level thing

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.180
I want to get across, because
some of the details about GPU

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.360
execution and how you write
a softmax kernel, that's

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.580
going to change,
and maybe you even

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:01.090
want to just rely on the
Torch compile auto JIT thing.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:02.590
But the fact that
you should profile

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:05.007
isn't really going to change
no matter what the tools are.

00:08:05.007 --> 00:08:07.750
So I want you to
internalize that idea

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:09.670
that you should be
always profiling

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:13.720
if you want to be writing
high performance code.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.070
And really, there's a
limit to the theory.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.040
I think systems is
part of this course

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.730
that you can reason
about pretty well.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:21.647
Architecture is somewhat
hard to reason about,

00:08:21.647 --> 00:08:25.190
and you can really think about
the roofline model and so on.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:27.812
But how fast is your
matrix multiply?

00:08:27.812 --> 00:08:29.770
Well, maybe that depends
on the library version

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:31.559
or your hardware,
like which things are

00:08:31.559 --> 00:08:32.809
bottlenecking for what reason.

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.140
There's all sorts of
microcode, things that you

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:37.039
don't really fully know.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:38.950
And so you have to,
in the end, have

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.750
to do end to end
benchmarking whenever

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:43.860
you're developing these things.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:45.970
So I'm going to have
an example computation.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.465
This is the simplest
thing that we

00:08:47.465 --> 00:08:49.590
can run compared to all
the things that you all are

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:51.390
doing in your assignment one.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.290
But I'm going to run
a very simple MLP.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.330
It's going to have
128 dimensions.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:56.950
It's going to have 16 layers.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:58.413
It's going to have
some batch size,

00:08:58.413 --> 00:08:59.830
and it's going to
have five steps.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:01.622
I'm going to just do
forwards and backwards

00:09:01.622 --> 00:09:03.840
for five different steps here.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.480
And just to make the code
clear, it's something like this.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.080
I'm going to define
an MLP model,

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.680
and I'll show you
that in a moment here,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.780
and then I'll define a
random Gaussian input,

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.920
and then I'll run it for five
steps in that last case, where

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.630
I compute some forward, and
then I compute a backwards,

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.260
and then I return
the result, which

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.490
is just the mean of
the output of my MLP.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:25.845
There's not even losses.

00:09:25.845 --> 00:09:26.470
It's so simple.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.300
It's just you run
the MLP forward

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:29.890
and I just average
pool at the end.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:33.300
And then the MLP is
just the simplest thing.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.130
You can also imagine
here it's just

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:38.400
a bunch of linear layers stacked
on top of each other, which

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.490
is this bit.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.420
And then I've got
a value in between.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.858
So this is just linear
value, so on and so forth.

00:09:45.858 --> 00:09:47.400
Everything is nice
and square, right?

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.340
So hopefully, this
is a very simple MLP

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.940
that you all feel
pretty comfortable with.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:55.830
And then let's go back.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:56.660
Yes.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:57.690
Oh, sorry.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.360
I want to go back up to here.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.610
OK, good.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.212
And so now I have this MLP
code that I want to run.

00:10:04.212 --> 00:10:05.670
And now I'm going
to do two things.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:08.003
I'm going to benchmark, so
I'm going to do some timings.

00:10:08.003 --> 00:10:10.920
So I want to know how long
does this function take to run.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.770
And then I'll do
profiling, which

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.770
is to go inside the
function and ask, where am I

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.420
spending all of my time?

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.650
So let's start
with benchmarking.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:23.210
So benchmarking is just the
measurement of wall clock time

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.490
of performing these operations.

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.860
And I'm only looking for
the end-to-end execution

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.800
time of, in this
case, my MLP function.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.753
And there are some
subtleties to this.

00:10:32.753 --> 00:10:34.670
You're sitting there and
you're like, why am I

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.200
being told how to invoke--

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.600
I don't know-- the
time it function?

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:41.200
But you do have to be a
little bit careful about how

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.268
you measure times.

00:10:42.268 --> 00:10:44.060
And I think if you're
not paying attention,

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:47.620
you will run into these pitfalls
when you do assignment two.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:49.520
And so what are
we doing this for?

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:51.410
We're going to compare
implementations later.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:54.640
We're going to compare our
Triton to our handwritten C++,

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.825
to PyTorch's implementation,
and Torch compile.

00:10:57.825 --> 00:10:59.200
And we want to
know, was it worth

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.910
it to write that CUDA kernel?

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.320
And we'd also like
to understand,

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.370
when I make my matrix
multiplies bigger,

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:05.810
how much slower does it get?

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:08.590
So we'd like to do some
empirical benchmarking of those.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:11.020
So throughout this
lecture, I'm going

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:12.940
to be using this
benchmark function.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:16.540
And that's going to be a wrapper
function, I'll step through it.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.672
Benchmark is going to
do the following things.

00:11:18.672 --> 00:11:20.380
It's going to have a
function that I want

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:21.622
to benchmark, which is run.

00:11:21.622 --> 00:11:24.080
And then I'm going to do some
number of warm up iterations.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.026
And then I'll do some
number of trials.

00:11:27.026 --> 00:11:30.820
And you might wonder, OK,
so what's this warm up thing

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:31.880
that we're doing here?

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.670
Well, one thing that's
really important

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:37.390
is when you first run
your PyTorch code,

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:40.620
and let's say it dispatches
something to the GPU,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.270
it might look very fast
and transparent to you,

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:44.700
but that very first
time something

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.220
is executed, in the background
machine code is being compiled.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.130
That code instruction might
be being sent to the GPU.

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:54.750
There's all sorts of things that
happen to initialize your code.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.720
And so you always want to
do some warm up iteration

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.060
to make sure that you're not
measuring the startup speed.

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:03.900
Instead, you want to measure
kind of the steady state speed.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.480
If you're running thousands
and thousands of iterations,

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.370
what you're interested
in is that part,

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:12.840
not necessarily how fast can
you do on the fly compilation

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.912
of your CUDA code.

00:12:14.912 --> 00:12:16.450
So that's why we have warm up.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.230
And you should always
have a bit of warm up.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.350
And then another thing that's
really important, and I'll

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.150
get to this once we
get to the profiler,

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.820
is you want to call this thing
called Torch CUDA synchronize.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:28.690
What is that?

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.530
Well, the GPU and
the CPU are basically

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.450
two independent compute
units in your computer.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:37.202
And they can basically
run kind of independently.

00:12:37.202 --> 00:12:38.660
And so the execution
model is going

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:41.160
to be this, Python
code that I have here,

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.710
this lives on the CPU.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.330
And when I run
something, it's going

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:47.653
to dispatch a bunch of
CUDA kernels to the GPU.

00:12:47.653 --> 00:12:49.320
It says, please run
these things for me.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:52.110
And the GPU will go off
and execute those things,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.180
and the CPU will actually
go on and keep running.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.000
It doesn't wait for those
CUDA executions to stop.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:00.110
And so, that's a great feature
for writing high performance

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:03.260
code, but you should hopefully
see the immediate problem

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.580
if you want to do benchmarking.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:05.990
If you're benchmarking
and you've

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.270
got this model where the
GPU runs off in the side

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.110
and your CPU is doing
something different,

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:13.130
you're actually not measuring
the GPU execution time.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.500
So Torch CUDA
synchronize basically

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.110
says, all right, let's make
sure that the GPU and CPU are

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:19.080
in the same state.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.480
And there's no queued
things running,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.140
and that we're kind of at the
same point in terms of the code

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.350
that's being executed.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:28.463
And now, also, the GPU and CPU
are kind of in the same state,

00:13:28.463 --> 00:13:29.880
and I'm going to
time it for real.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.537
And I'm going to time something
for some number of times.

00:13:32.537 --> 00:13:34.370
And I'm going to run
the computation, which,

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.660
in this case, is
the sleep command.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.160
I'm going to do it three times.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.560
And since I'm trying to
sleep for 50 milliseconds,

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.840
that's the time that I'm
going to get at the end.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:47.210
So I do time that
time three times.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:51.620
And of course here, I'm also
calling torch.cuda.synchronize

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:54.530
at the end of run to make
sure that the GPU and CPU

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:55.370
states are the same.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:56.870
So if the CPU is
running ahead, it's

00:13:56.870 --> 00:13:58.850
going to wait for
the GPU execution

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:01.700
to actually finish
here, and vice versa.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:04.550
And so now, I finished and
then I'm going to average,

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:08.510
because each single measurement
might be fluctuating because

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:10.672
of things like thermal
properties of the GPU,

00:14:10.672 --> 00:14:13.130
and so you want to take multiple
replicates, take the mean,

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:13.980
and return that.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:16.190
That's our benchmarking code.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:17.250
Very simple.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.160
But remember the two
important pieces here.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:21.300
Always do a warm up.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.600
Make sure to call
CUDA synchronize.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.470
If you do those,
it's very simple.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.650
If you forget to do those,
you'll get pretty crazy numbers.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.320
You'll get that your big matrix
multiply finished instantly,

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.090
which is definitely not true.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.430
So now, we can do
some benchmarking

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:37.050
of matrix multiplies.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.160
I'm going to walk
through some of these.

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:41.710
They're just putting numbers
to things that we already know,

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.960
but I want to just walk
through it and make sure we're

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.680
on the same page here.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.620
Si I ran this on the
class A100s, I have GPUs,

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:54.570
I'm going to do matrix
multiplies over these sizes,

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.060
and then I'm going
to go and collect

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.360
a whole bunch of
matrix multiply timings

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:02.400
for each of these
dimensions, stepping

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.960
through this benchmark result.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.750
And so, we see--

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:10.838
as we expect, super linear
scaling of our runtimes as we

00:15:10.838 --> 00:15:11.880
increase the matrix size.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.160
Of course, at the smallest
sizes, like 1024 and 2048,

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.580
we actually see that the
times don't grow at all

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:21.000
because there's constant
factor overhead in just doing

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.150
these matrix multiplies.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:25.990
These numbers have to get
shipped from the CPU to the GPU,

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:29.250
there's overhead in
launching the kernel,

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.490
and so it's not
the case that it's

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.530
super linear all the way to 0.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.130
But once the matrices
get big enough,

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:36.560
we see exactly the
kind of scaling

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.840
that we expect to see with
our matrix multiplies.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.240
OK.

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.290
So hopefully straightforward.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.810
Now, let's try to
benchmark our MLP.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.610
So what are we going to do?

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.140
We're going to make
our MLP bigger.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:50.627
We're going to have
256 dimensions.

00:15:50.627 --> 00:15:51.960
We're going to have four layers.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:53.580
Batch size of 256.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.130
Take two steps.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.270
And so what's the time
that it takes to do that?

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:00.750
Well, it's going to take
6.2 seconds to do that.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:03.510
And now I could do
some basic things.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.570
I can scale the number
of steps from 2 to 5,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.370
and I can benchmark
all of those.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:13.560
And I'll get 2, 3,
4, and then 5 steps.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:15.990
And unlike in the
matrix multiply case,

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:17.900
if I'm scaling the
number of steps,

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:21.020
or the number of forward and
backward passes on my MLP,

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:23.400
what do I expect the
runtime to behave like?

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.340
Well, I expect linear scaling.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.200
And that's kind of what we see.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:31.280
There's about five
seconds per MLP execution,

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:35.020
and we see it's about n
times 5 for the runtime

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:38.530
of the end-to-end object here.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:41.740
OK, let me see if I can
set the thing that's

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.040
being monitored here.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:43.820
Oh, nope, I can't.

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:45.050
OK, I'm going to zoom
out a little bit.

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:45.890
Sorry about that.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.160
OK, now we can also scale
the number of layers

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:53.300
from 2, 3, 4 to 5, and
what does that give us?

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:56.620
Well, it gives us
increasing runtimes.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.270
Once again, linear in
the number of layers.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:03.490
This time, once again, one
layer takes about five seconds,

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:04.670
a little bit less than that.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.569
And so we get about four
times-- actually four times

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:09.040
the number of layers.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:11.210
And linear scaling
shows up again.

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:12.230
Unsurprising, right?

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:14.319
So both steps and
layers obviously

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.910
have linear relationships
with the runtime,

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:20.275
and that is exactly what we
end up seeing at the end here.

00:17:20.275 --> 00:17:22.900
I'm going to skip the batch size
thing, because this is getting

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:25.690
a little bit unwieldy in
terms of the amount of things

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:27.069
that are being tracked here.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:28.750
OK, all right.

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:31.750
So that's the end of
this benchmarking bit.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.290
We can make this
nice function that

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.730
does a little bit of warm
up, does CUDA synchronize,

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:39.100
and we can measure the runtime
of anything that we want.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:39.820
And this is good.

00:17:39.820 --> 00:17:41.740
And you should do this all
the time in your code right.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:43.260
You can measure
how long it takes

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:45.720
for your new fancy
architecture to run.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:49.030
But then I think if you
want to fix some problems,

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:50.950
benchmarking is a very
coarse grained tool.

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:52.600
It tells you that
your code is slow,

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:56.050
but it doesn't tell you where
the time is being spent.

00:17:56.050 --> 00:18:01.170
And so what we would like to
do is instead do profiling.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:04.080
And so this is going to be a
much more fine grained object

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.000
that we're going to want to do.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.450
And so profiling is really
nice, because it not only helps

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.910
you see where the time is
being spent, which functions,

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.250
but when you look at
what you're calling,

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:18.360
usually you interact with the
PyTorch interface, the parts

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:19.450
of PyTorch that you call.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.170
But beneath PyTorch,
there's this whole universe

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:24.160
of CUDA stuff
that's being called.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.330
And when you run a
profiler, you can actually

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:28.970
see, all the way to
the low level calls,

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:30.330
what is actually being called.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:32.270
And so you can get a
much nicer intuition

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:36.420
for how the program is actually
being executed on the hardware.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:38.690
And so we'll step
through profiling

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:42.680
a few simple functions, and then
get a little bit of intuition

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:44.750
about what is happening.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:46.640
And so one of the
things that is nice

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.000
is that if you want
basic profiling,

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.770
PyTorch has a very
nice kind of built

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:53.660
in profiler that you can use.

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:57.350
And this will allow you to not
leave the Python PyTorch world

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:00.930
and get some fairly
reasonable looking outputs.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.480
And so I've profiled
some functions here,

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.740
and you can see the
output of this as well.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:10.580
And so I've taken the
sleep example from before.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.230
And here is the sleep function.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.470
And when we profile
the sleep function,

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:17.460
the profile function
looks something like this.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.110
I have a warm up again, I
have Torch CUDA synchronize,

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.780
and then I call the
profiler, and I'm tracking

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:27.290
both CPU and the GPU times.

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:30.960
And then I run something,
and then I synchronize again,

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.680
and I print out the average
table across all the time.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:37.130
So I go back now.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:40.370
So now, I'm going to
profile the sleep function.

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:43.800
And if we look at what's
happening, what happens here?

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.950
Well, 100% of the
time is being spent

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:48.590
on something called CUDA device
synchronize because there's

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.250
no GPU work being done.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.490
This is just a no op,
it's kind of a silly thing

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:53.520
to be profiling.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:56.250
And so now let's look at
something kind non-trivial.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:59.120
So let's look at
this basic operation

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:02.370
here of adding two matrices.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:05.360
So I defined add function
that takes in an A and B

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:07.190
and adds them together.

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.010
And this is a
helper function that

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:12.170
instantiates two random
Gaussian matrices

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:15.540
and then invokes whatever's
in the operation argument.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:19.650
So this is adding to 2048
sized matrices together.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:21.370
So now I'm going
to profile this,

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:23.320
and I'm going to
call the profiler.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:27.140
And I'll get back something that
looks like this block over here.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:28.900
So this is what I get back.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.990
And I'm going to have to zoom
back out, because this is not

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:34.130
going to be all right.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.245
Is this visible from the back?

00:20:36.245 --> 00:20:37.870
Can someone give me
a thumbs up if it's

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:39.010
visible from the back or not?

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:39.950
OK, good, good, good.

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:41.000
Or thumbs down if it's not.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:41.500
All right.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:47.470
So when we call the add function
in Python, this is kind of all

00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:50.440
that we interact with, this
add function, A plus B. That's

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:51.410
all we think about.

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:53.770
But actually, underneath
here, underneath the iceberg,

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.450
so to speak, there's a
lot more that happens.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:58.280
So this gets
dispatched to the GPU.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:00.070
And first, there's
this thing called

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:04.220
aten, which is the C
interface for PyTorch,

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:06.340
and so this wrapper gets
called and it says, OK,

00:21:06.340 --> 00:21:07.730
I'm going to add some numbers.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:10.700
This is what's being called,
that's the outer wrapper.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:13.990
And then that dispatches
to a particular kernel

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:18.070
called vectorize elementwise
kernel 4, at native CUDA vector

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:19.415
add da, da, da, da, da.

00:21:19.415 --> 00:21:22.150
And this is the thing that's
actually doing the adding.

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:24.270
And then there's this,
also, other thing

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.860
called CUDA launch kernel
that's taking some time.

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:30.300
And this is actually the
CPU is taking the command

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:31.930
and sending it over to the GPU.

00:21:31.930 --> 00:21:34.240
That's the kernel launch,
and that takes some time.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.790
And then finally, the
CUDA device synchronizes,

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:39.120
we're waiting for the GPU
to finish and send things

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.010
back to us, and that
also takes some time.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:43.830
The mere act of having a
synchronization barrier

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:45.540
is going to cost us some time.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:49.180
And so we basically have the
time total in the end here,

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:55.540
1.4 milliseconds on the CPU and
17 microseconds on the CUDA.

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:59.230
So this is really fast on
the GPU, slower on the CPU.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:03.180
And if we're looking at the
CPU time that's being spent,

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:07.052
which is the self-CPU time,
we see that the C++ interface,

00:22:07.052 --> 00:22:09.510
or the C interface, is actually
the thing that's costing us

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.630
a whole bunch of CPU time, and
their overhead to doing anything

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.960
where we're sending
stuff over to the GPU.

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.650
So that's the add
function, and we see what's

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:20.540
happening under the hood.

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:23.040
Same story here if I want
to do a matrix multiply.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:26.420
So I'm doing A multiplied by
B, so this is a matrix multiply

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:30.000
of A and B. I'm doing
2048 matrices once again,

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.120
and then I do profiling.

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:35.400
Now, this time,
I see 810 matmul,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.910
so this is saying like this
is the lower level interface

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.920
to do matrix multiplies.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:42.470
And this is going to
dispatch the Cutlass, which

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:46.770
is NVIDIA's high performance
matrix multiply CUDA library.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:50.180
And then it's dispatching to a
very particular Cutlass kernel,

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:52.370
which is going to
have some tile size.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:54.000
The names are truncated here.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.570
I'll show you a more
detailed version in a minute.

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:59.180
This is basically pointing
towards a very particular set

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:03.450
of tile sizes and the
number of blocks, and so on.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:05.390
And so this thing
is parametrized,

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.790
and that's actually doing
the matrix multiply.

00:23:07.790 --> 00:23:10.590
And once again, we see the same
two things at the bottom here,

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:15.370
the kernel launch and the
synchronization of CUDA devices.

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:19.430
And you can see, once again,
the CPU time CUDA time split.

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:21.100
And we're spending
way more time in CUDA

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.100
because matrix multiplies
do take more time

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:25.640
than just adding two vectors.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:29.390
Any questions so far?

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:30.680
I can pause for a moment here.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:34.750
I think I've just been going
very quickly and on my own

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:35.630
through the profiler.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:37.880
So if anyone has questions,
I can stop for a moment.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.060
If not, I can keep going.

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:41.046
OK.

00:23:41.046 --> 00:23:42.550
Oh, yes.

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:45.740
In this case, our CUDA time
is greater than our CPU time,

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.850
but we did have a
barrier that said to--

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:51.620
for the CPU to wait
and try to synchronize.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:54.420
And so by that, shouldn't
the CPU time always

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:56.420
be at least the same
amount [INAUDIBLE] of time?

00:23:56.420 --> 00:23:57.220
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:57.220 --> 00:23:59.500
We're all counting the
time that this [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:03.610
Yeah, I don't think this
counts the [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:04.120
Cool.

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:04.580
Oh, yes.

00:24:04.580 --> 00:24:05.540
Sorry, there's two questions.

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:06.540
There's all [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:08.440
Is there any
particular reason why

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:13.080
when we switch from adding to
matmul the CPU time went down?

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:16.230
Is there a reason why when we
go from adding to matmul the CPU

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:16.980
time goes down?

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.100
That I am not sure,
to be entirely honest.

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.570
Yes.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:23.280
Is there some overhead
in the profiler

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:27.000
that can distort [INAUDIBLE]
compared to letting it

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:27.990
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.840
Is there overhead
in the profiler

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:33.240
that can distort things compared
to running it in the real world?

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.480
Yes, there is overhead
in the profiler.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:37.960
The barriers will do that.

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:40.210
I'll show you a more advanced
profiler from NVIDIA,

00:24:40.210 --> 00:24:41.835
and you can add things
like annotations

00:24:41.835 --> 00:24:45.430
that will also slightly distort
the timings, but not by much.

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:47.400
The really large scale
things that you see

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:50.880
aren't going to be really
distorted by the profiler.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.830
So if you're looking at
micro timings, yes, probably.

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:57.930
But a lot of the things that
we care about in the class, no.

00:24:57.930 --> 00:24:58.797
Yes.

00:24:58.797 --> 00:25:00.880
Just to make sure I'm
interpreting this correctly,

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:08.910
so for the adding case, is 98%
of the CPU being utilized over

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:10.760
the time period that it's--

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:12.530
for the second time period?

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:13.183
That's right.

00:25:13.183 --> 00:25:14.850
Yeah, so this is the
percentage of time.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:18.320
As you can see, that the actual
millisecond time that aten::add

00:25:18.320 --> 00:25:23.990
was actually executing in
some capacity on the CPU.

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:27.080
I don't think the CPU
[INAUDIBLE] percent

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:29.550
of what the CPU is doing.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:30.090
[INAUDIBLE]

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:30.680
Yeah, that's right.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.263
This is the time of
the CPU is active,

00:25:32.263 --> 00:25:34.340
not percentage utilization,
if that's-- yeah,

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:37.040
so this is not like the
total amount of CPU flops

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:37.770
or something.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:39.470
This is the total
percentage of time

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:42.890
that the CPU is doing something.

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:43.580
Yes.

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:45.170
OK, cool.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:46.250
All right.

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:48.480
Here's another
example of a matmul.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:50.430
So this is a different
dimensionality.

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:51.230
So this is a--

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:55.850
I'm multiplying 128
dimensional matrix here.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:59.030
So 128 by 128, much smaller.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:04.250
And you'll actually see that now
it's actually directly executing

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:08.830
this different command,
it's executing XMMA GMM.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:12.640
GMM is a matrix multiply type.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:13.750
And this is float32.

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:17.928
Float32, you can see from the
naming of this kernel what's

00:26:17.928 --> 00:26:20.470
actually happening here, which
is that this is a tiled matrix

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:22.670
multiply of some kind.

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.380
And it's not going
through Cutlass,

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:26.900
it's executing this
particular command directly.

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.900
And so for a small
matrix multiply,

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:32.020
you see that it's dispatching
to a different kernel now.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:36.040
So you can see the complexity
of matrix multiply.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:38.630
When we're operating at this
high level of abstraction,

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:40.930
we just think of matrix
multiply as a single thing.

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:42.947
We call A at B, and we're done.

00:26:42.947 --> 00:26:45.280
But underneath the hood,
depending on the dimensionality

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:48.080
that you have, depending on
the hardware that you have,

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:52.420
it will actually dispatch to
very different matrix multiply

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:54.170
primitives under the hood.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:57.460
And that will actually manifest
in very, very different

00:26:57.460 --> 00:26:59.210
performance characteristics.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:02.180
And so one fun tip
is Torch compile,

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:04.060
which I will talk about
later, actually has

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:07.440
an option to macrobenchmark
the matrix multiply performance

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:09.780
on your hardware, and then
it will actually then pick

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:13.320
the highest performing
matrix multiply subroutines

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.990
for your model,
which in the past

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.700
I found gives you 10%
speed up for free.

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:21.360
It's very cool that
optimizing for these things

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:25.170
actually gives you free
gains out in the real world.

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:29.850
OK, so that's another
matmul example.

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:31.960
And so the cool thing
about the profiler,

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:34.150
compared to just the
raw benchmarking,

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:37.480
is we can now see which CUDA
kernels are being called.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:41.100
We can see that different
sizes of matrices

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:43.170
lead to different CUDA kernels.

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:50.460
And we see cutlass_80simtt_sgemm
is this cutlass linear algebra

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:54.660
library, and it tells us
things like the tile size.

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:58.800
So far, these operations
are very boring, in a way.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:00.870
Like matrix multiplies and adds.

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:02.230
They're basically one to one.

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:05.610
You have operation
on the CPU side,

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:07.880
it translates to
a GPU operation,

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.360
and it just gets shipped over.

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.360
So there's just a
single operation

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.500
in all of these that
does anything on the GPU.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:16.920
So I want to look at some
more complicated operations,

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:21.170
two more of these, that
have more compound behavior.

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:23.990
So what I want to do
now is I want to do--

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:27.050
I want to look at this
operation called torch.cdist,

00:28:27.050 --> 00:28:30.240
and this is computing
for two sets of matrices,

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:33.450
the pairwise Euclidean distance
between two sets of vectors.

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:35.960
So this is going to be
a big distance matrix

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:38.910
computation between A's
and B's that I want.

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:40.310
So that's cdist.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:43.620
And so this is obviously a much
more complicated operation.

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:46.100
If you want to compute
Euclidean distances,

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.360
you're going to need to
compute dot products,

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:50.540
you're going to need to
compute square roots.

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:54.260
And we're going to see
that once we compute cdist.

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:59.390
So now here is the
profiled output of cdist.

00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:03.040
So we see that this
Torch Python command

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:07.840
does map in the C interface
to some of lower level cdist.

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.240
So this is aten::cdist,
which then maps

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:12.113
to aten::euclidean_dist.

00:29:12.113 --> 00:29:14.530
And then this will decompose
into a whole bunch of things,

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:18.640
like aten::matmul, aten::pow,
and then some because these are

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:21.370
all primitives that you're going
to need in order to actually

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:26.140
to compute the Euclidean
distances between all

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:27.020
of your vectors.

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:29.530
And when you for each
one of these matrix

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:33.640
multiplies and concatenation
and taking the powers,

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:37.420
you have a corresponding
CUDA command

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:38.980
that is being called here.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:41.720
We have GMM, which we've
become familiar with,

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:43.280
so this is a matrix multiply.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:48.730
It's taking 78% of our
compute time on the GPU.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:53.120
We've got copies and
concatenation of arrays.

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:57.020
This takes 6% of
the execution time.

00:29:57.020 --> 00:30:00.250
And then this vectorized
element wise kernel,

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:04.920
which is taking the power,
takes 5% of the GPU time,

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:06.760
and 3% goes to the sum.

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:09.600
So now we get this very
nice low level breakdown

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.080
of where my GPU is
spending all of its time.

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:15.000
And from this, I
can get some sense

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:18.810
of where maybe I should
spend my time optimizing.

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:21.190
Maybe I think I can
optimize my matrix multiply.

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:24.810
That would be great, because
that's 70%+ of the time spent

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.910
in the GPU.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:30.150
The final example-- the
final two examples, sorry,

00:30:30.150 --> 00:30:32.340
that I want to talk about
is gelu and softmax.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.840
So these will be our running--
oh sorry, there's a question.

00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:39.270
Yeah, what's the CPU [INAUDIBLE]
the computation going on?

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:42.990
So I will maybe answering that
question in a few minutes,

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:44.610
because there's a
cooler profiler that

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:46.090
shows you a much nicer picture.

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:47.640
And so I can
gesticulate here, but I

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:51.000
think it'll be better to
show that with pictures.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:56.250
So I'm going to talk about
now the gelu and the softmax.

00:30:56.250 --> 00:30:59.810
So the gelu is going to
be our running example

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:01.110
throughout the class.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:02.802
So this is a non-linearity.

00:31:02.802 --> 00:31:04.760
If you remember, it's
the Gaussian error unit--

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:07.490
Gaussian error linear unit.

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:13.370
And that's going to be a product
of a tanh and a exponential,

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:14.900
if I remember right.

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:18.665
And so we're going to have
all sorts of operations.

00:31:18.665 --> 00:31:20.540
So we're going to add
A and B, and then we're

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:24.740
going to call gelu, simulating
the linear plus nonlinear

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:28.400
structure that we
might have in our MLP.

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:32.100
And so we see, once again,
basically the same mapping.

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:34.470
We see aten::add
corresponding to A plus B.

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:36.630
And then we have
the CUDA equivalent.

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.870
And then we have,
actually, a gelu function

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:41.850
implemented in CUDA, which
is all the way down here.

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:44.900
And that takes about
33% of the compute.

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:45.480
OK.

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:46.500
Fairly reasonable.

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:48.390
And then we have, once
again, the softmax.

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:51.410
I won't go through all
of these in gory detail

00:31:51.410 --> 00:31:54.540
since they all start to
look the same after a while.

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:56.930
But the thing to really point
out that I think is cool

00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:58.810
is that a lot of these
really core primitives

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:02.980
like softmax and gelu, there's
just kernels written for them.

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:06.410
So it's not like the GPU is
executing the basic primitives.

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:09.170
There's a fused operator
that computes all of this.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:11.950
So there's no back and
forth between CPU and GPU

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:13.600
for all of these.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:15.760
So OK, I mentioned
before that I was

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:18.723
going to answer this question
of what the CPU was doing.

00:32:18.723 --> 00:32:20.140
And so let's think
about something

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:21.380
a little more sophisticated.

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:25.300
I had that MLP example that I
started with for benchmarking,

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.800
and I would let's say
like to optimize the MLP,

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.210
make it run really fast.

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:31.460
So how can we do that.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:36.920
Well, ideally we would profile
this in a nice fine grained way.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.460
So if we use the
Torch profiler this

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:40.820
is kind of what we would get.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:43.370
If you remember the MLP
there's stacked linear layers.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:45.130
There's a forward
and a backward.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:49.370
And you see roughly there's this
backward thing that's happening.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:50.810
There is a matrix multiply.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:52.420
There's linear and then

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:56.280
there's accumulate grad
operation for the backward.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.197
And here's the matrix
multiply kernel.

00:32:58.197 --> 00:33:00.280
And then there's only 10
things that can fit here.

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:02.620
So I think this gets cut
off at a certain point.

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:03.877
But this is nice.

00:33:03.877 --> 00:33:05.460
It does tell you
that most of the time

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.010
is being spent in the matmuls.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:11.100
But you do kind of wonder, where
does all the rest of the time

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:11.680
go.

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:16.030
And why does only 31%
of my time stay here.

00:33:16.030 --> 00:33:18.010
And where's the 60% here.

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.520
It's a aten::mm but there's
no corresponding kernel.

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:22.210
This is a little bit mysterious.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:25.810
And for something that's
very complex module,

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:28.530
this is not a very
good visualization.

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:31.890
And so for that, I think
we have to actually get out

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:34.830
a real grown up profiler.

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:37.170
And you will have
to-- or we will

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:40.860
ask you to look at
this thing, which

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:42.900
is NVIDIA's Nsight systems.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:46.530
And this is the kind of
NVIDIA's detailed way

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:50.110
of looking at GPU
behavior and performance.

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:52.410
And so we will actually
see exactly what is

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:55.380
happening as we run this MLP.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:57.900
So actually, in the
back, can you see--

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:00.520
I don't know-- this
tiny text over here?

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:01.020
Thumbs up?

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:01.390
OK.

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:01.780
All right.

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:03.822
If you can see it, then
I'm not going to zoom in.

00:34:03.822 --> 00:34:06.450
But it does seem
small, even from here.

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:10.510
All right, so basically,
if we look here,

00:34:10.510 --> 00:34:12.100
we see several different things.

00:34:12.100 --> 00:34:15.870
We see CUDA HW over here
and then we see threads.

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:18.300
And so this top half,
this CUDA part, this

00:34:18.300 --> 00:34:21.010
is what the GPU
is kind of doing.

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:25.929
And then in this threads part,
we see what the CPU is doing.

00:34:25.929 --> 00:34:28.949
And I can also pull
up the code, I think.

00:34:28.949 --> 00:34:32.290
Yes, the code here,
when I profiled it--

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:34.050
I've added a few annotations.

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.210
OK, this one I need
to zoom in for sure.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:39.370
OK, let's zoom.

00:34:39.370 --> 00:34:39.870
Excellent.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:41.130
All right.

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:46.035
So I've annotated the code with
this set of things that says--

00:34:46.035 --> 00:34:47.790
oh, let's see--

00:34:47.790 --> 00:34:55.800
NVTX, which basically
annotates my code with markers.

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:58.050
So when the profiler
comes in here,

00:34:58.050 --> 00:35:00.230
it will know that
this piece of code

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:02.430
belongs to a block
called define model.

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:06.990
And for example, this part that
says step range push and range

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:11.570
pop, this range here
from line 77 to line 55

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:14.560
should be annotated with
something that says step_step,

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:15.060
OK?

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:17.180
So I've added all of these
annotations in my code

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:20.180
before, calling on my profiler.

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:21.500
So let's go back here.

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:24.560
So now if we go to this
line that says NVTX,

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:28.190
we can see define model, which
is the thing that I wrapped

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:30.710
my model construction
call, and then I

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:34.320
see step 0, step 1, step
2, step 3, step 4, step 5.

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:37.410
So each step is now nicely
annotated in this profiler.

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:40.190
And we can see all of
the things that the model

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:43.020
is doing as it goes along.

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:44.990
And I'll start on this side.

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:47.902
One thing we see is
that this piece of code,

00:35:47.902 --> 00:35:49.110
it doesn't do very much work.

00:35:49.110 --> 00:35:50.990
It takes only 14 seconds.

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:53.260
So actually, most of the
time for the profiler

00:35:53.260 --> 00:35:54.740
is spent on overhead.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:57.520
So the part up
until roughly here

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:00.650
is things like just
loading the libraries,

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:01.820
and that takes a long time.

00:36:01.820 --> 00:36:05.060
It takes apparently 7.5 seconds
to just initialize everything.

00:36:05.060 --> 00:36:09.880
And then, at least on
the GPU, at 7.5 seconds

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:11.560
or so into the
program, it starts

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:13.070
actually building the model.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:15.400
And you see here, on
the memory footprint,

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:19.100
this is the place where now
memory is being allocated.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:23.360
And on the GPU memory, the
memory usage starts to grow Now,

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:25.370
the model is now
constructed at this point,

00:36:25.370 --> 00:36:29.240
and then step zero is where
the action starts to happen.

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:31.600
And so you were
asking earlier, what's

00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:35.660
happening between
the CPU and GPU?

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:40.030
And so how the execution
model of this works is here

00:36:40.030 --> 00:36:43.610
is step 0 on the CPU, and
I'm starting right here.

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:45.860
And here's the forward pass,
and this is layer zero.

00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:48.900
So let's just think
through what's happening.

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.450
As I said before, when
you first encounter

00:36:51.450 --> 00:36:54.540
or when you first call a
piece of code in PyTorch,

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:56.110
it doesn't just
directly execute.

00:36:56.110 --> 00:37:00.040
It will actually do things like,
on the fly, compile things.

00:37:00.040 --> 00:37:04.170
And so this thing, like runtime
triggered module loading,

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:08.760
is overhead work that's being
done in order to just initialize

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:12.420
the layer and the computation
and move various bits of code

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:13.860
into the GPU.

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:15.930
So this takes a long time.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:18.700
And then after this
layer zero is done,

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:23.472
now if I look at any slice here,
let's zoom in to selection,

00:37:23.472 --> 00:37:24.930
we'll see that each
of these layers

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:27.220
is really, really, really quick.

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:30.390
And what happens here is
when I highlight this layer

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.940
one over here on
the CPU side, notice

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:36.250
that that's not where layer
one is on the GPU side.

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:39.060
So as I said before,
the CPU and GPU

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:41.380
are two different
execution devices.

00:37:41.380 --> 00:37:44.400
So I start at layer zero,
I'm done with layer zero,

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:46.850
I start layer one, now
the CPU is actually

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:52.670
just sending all of the CUDA
commands, the CUDA kernels--

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:54.500
it's launching all the
CUDA kernels already

00:37:54.500 --> 00:37:55.850
to the GPU at this point.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:37:58.410
So when the CPU is saying,
I'm doing layer one,

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:01.328
what it's actually doing is it's
queuing commands into the GPU.

00:38:01.328 --> 00:38:02.870
It says, now run
this thing, next run

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:04.972
this thing, next
run this thing next.

00:38:04.972 --> 00:38:07.980
And so the CPU is running
way ahead of the GPU,

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:11.910
and by the time layer one
starts executing on the GPU,

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:14.570
actually, we're already
at layer nine on the CPU.

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:17.900
The CPU is running way ahead,
and there's basically a queue

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:20.900
that the CPU
maintains where it's

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:25.848
sending a fixed number of a
kernel CUDA kernels to the GPU.

00:38:25.848 --> 00:38:27.390
And so once you hit
that queue depth,

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:29.040
it's going to stop
running ahead.

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:30.582
But until that point,
it's just going

00:38:30.582 --> 00:38:33.945
to keep going and going
and going as far as it can.

00:38:33.945 --> 00:38:36.380
And in this case,
this does become--

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:39.480
I'm going to zoom out again.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:41.690
Undo the zoom.

00:38:41.690 --> 00:38:43.220
There we go.

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:46.120
In this case, this kind
of gets a little extreme

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:51.280
because if I zoom out once more,
notice how, in these steps,

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:54.560
I'm running way ahead,
like the step zero is here,

00:38:54.560 --> 00:38:55.670
step two is here.

00:38:55.670 --> 00:38:58.210
This was step one, which
basically took no time at all.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.640
Step two is here, so
the CPU is basically

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:05.860
running one entire step forward
and backward ahead of the GPU.

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:10.150
One interesting thing that you
might do is if you're writing

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:12.665
various code for training
a language model,

00:39:12.665 --> 00:39:14.290
one normal thing that
you might do is--

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:15.760
let's go back to the code--

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:20.770
I might do something like print
my losses in between iterations.

00:39:20.770 --> 00:39:23.440
This seems like it should
have no effect on what

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:24.640
the GPU is doing.

00:39:24.640 --> 00:39:26.390
You're like, well,
it's a print statement.

00:39:26.390 --> 00:39:27.730
How much could it do?

00:39:27.730 --> 00:39:29.270
If you think about
it for a moment,

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:33.260
this will have big impacts on
the execution layout on the GPU,

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.390
because in order to print
this statement right,

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:37.240
this print statement
happens on the CPU

00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:39.350
and the CPU needs
to get the loss.

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:42.573
That means it needs to wait for
the GPU to compute that loss,

00:39:42.573 --> 00:39:43.990
and so let's look
at what happens.

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:47.580
So here, as I said,
step four on the CPU

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:49.660
happens way before
the GPU equivalent.

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:50.800
Now, let's switch back.

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:53.010
Now, this is the version
that I profiled where

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:54.250
it has the print statement.

00:39:54.250 --> 00:39:59.340
And then now I zoom
into selection here.

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:02.940
Now, see how step
one and step two

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:05.560
are basically kind
of synchronized now.

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:08.738
Because I have to wait for
the loss to get computed.

00:40:08.738 --> 00:40:11.280
And you look at this and you
say, oh, but it's still a little

00:40:11.280 --> 00:40:12.130
offset right.

00:40:12.130 --> 00:40:15.220
Step two-- step one isn't
exactly aligned with each other,

00:40:15.220 --> 00:40:18.010
so now let's zoom
back in and see, OK,

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:19.960
what happened to
step one on the CPU?

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:24.450
Well, basically, the endpoint
of step one on the CPU

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.810
is also kind of where the
optimizer step starts.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:31.410
So by the time that
forward is done--

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:33.370
sorry, this CUDA stream
synchronize the thing.

00:40:33.370 --> 00:40:36.220
So this CUDA stream
synchronize command on the CPU,

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:38.550
this is basically saying,
I'm just waiting for the GPU,

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:39.790
because I can't run ahead.

00:40:39.790 --> 00:40:41.540
I'm waiting for this
loss to be computed

00:40:41.540 --> 00:40:43.140
and to be sent back to me.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.900
So this is a dummy
operation where

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:47.300
it's saying CPU waits, waits,
waits, waits, waits, waits,

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:50.430
waits, well, the
backward step is done,

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.050
so now I can print the loss.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:55.310
I've printed the loss, now the
CPU can start running ahead.

00:40:55.310 --> 00:40:58.250
And it does run ahead and starts
sending step two stuff now.

00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:02.160
And then, well, once it hits
here, it's run out of commands.

00:41:02.160 --> 00:41:03.510
It's waiting for the loss again.

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:05.550
CUDA synchronize, wait,
wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:06.930
Backward step is done.

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:08.070
Now I can print the loss.

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:09.892
Now I run ahead again.

00:41:09.892 --> 00:41:13.250
So in this case, the GPU
is still, essentially,

00:41:13.250 --> 00:41:14.978
full utilization in both cases.

00:41:14.978 --> 00:41:16.520
But in extreme cases,
where let's say

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:18.312
you're printing tons
of stuff all the time,

00:41:18.312 --> 00:41:20.520
actually, you're going to
introduce a CPU bottleneck,

00:41:20.520 --> 00:41:22.280
because the GPU has
to-- or the CPU has

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:24.620
to keep waiting for the
GPU, and it can't launch

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:26.930
the kernels ahead of time.

00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:29.930
So that's kind of
a really cool thing

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:34.198
that you can see with the
profiler, the CPU versus GPU,

00:41:34.198 --> 00:41:35.990
and they're actually
different devices that

00:41:35.990 --> 00:41:37.230
communicate to each other.

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.170
It's not a single
unified object,

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:40.750
and you wouldn't
see that unless you

00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:44.140
started to look at some of
these more advanced profilers.

00:41:44.140 --> 00:41:47.520
Any question about
that set of things?

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:50.830
Cool.

00:41:50.830 --> 00:41:52.000
OK.

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:56.890
And the other thing that I want
to show you is the profiler

00:41:56.890 --> 00:41:59.330
thing that I was
playing with before,

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:03.920
you can also generate very
similar views in NSIS as well,

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:07.870
where you select some range of
things that you want to-- let's

00:42:07.870 --> 00:42:08.657
do a warm up.

00:42:08.657 --> 00:42:11.240
I said we should, so we should
exclude the first couple steps.

00:42:11.240 --> 00:42:14.830
So we'll start at step three,
and we'll measure some steps.

00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:17.660
In this range, we could
take the kernels--

00:42:17.660 --> 00:42:19.340
this is what's doing
the computation--

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:22.060
and you can see that there's
actually many different kinds

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:22.970
of matrix multiply.

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:24.590
This is one matrix
multiply kernel,

00:42:24.590 --> 00:42:26.480
this is a different
matrix multiply kernel,

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:29.740
there's a different
vectorized element kernel.

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:31.630
And all of these are
taking different amounts

00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:32.810
of computation.

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:37.170
And we can take this and
we can say, oh, show me,

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:40.410
in the event's view, all of
the things that are happening.

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:43.380
And I can also see,
the stats view,

00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:46.720
all of the time that it takes--

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:49.000
wait, let's see.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:51.010
We want the average time?

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:52.120
No, we want-- sorry.

00:42:52.120 --> 00:42:58.170
The CUDA kernel
execution summary.

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:02.740
Yeah, we want the total
duration of the kernels.

00:43:02.740 --> 00:43:05.310
And so we can see
which kernels are

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:08.620
taking the most time in
aggregate across these views.

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:10.770
So this is actually a
very, very powerful tool

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:13.650
that can give you both the
aggregate view of what's slow

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.985
and what's fast, as well
as individual kernels that

00:43:15.985 --> 00:43:17.860
are being launched and
when they're launched,

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:21.900
and where the CPU commands
for that came from.

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:24.750
And I guess one final
side note here this

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:28.140
is one of the reasons why
it doesn't matter that we're

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:30.990
programming in Python and Python
is not a very high performance

00:43:30.990 --> 00:43:31.510
language.

00:43:31.510 --> 00:43:33.450
Because the CPU is
never the bottleneck,

00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:38.150
because the CPU can run ahead
and queue commands into the GPU.

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:43.490
And so this detaching or
this disconnecting aspect

00:43:43.490 --> 00:43:45.650
between the GPU
and the CPU is one

00:43:45.650 --> 00:43:47.900
of the key reasons why we
can use this nice high level

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:49.790
programming language
and yet still get

00:43:49.790 --> 00:43:54.410
of full utilization
out of our GPUs.

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:55.070
Cool.

00:43:55.070 --> 00:43:56.090
OK.

00:43:56.090 --> 00:43:59.040
Any questions before
I switch back to this?

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:02.360
Because I'm going to leave
NSIS forever for this lecture,

00:44:02.360 --> 00:44:04.700
at this point.

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:05.520
Cool, yeah.

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:07.020
But you'll get to play
with it in assignment two.

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:08.395
And I think you'll
appreciate it,

00:44:08.395 --> 00:44:12.320
because it gives you a really
interesting view into what

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:15.710
your hardware is actually doing
to make these language models

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:16.970
train.

00:44:16.970 --> 00:44:19.012
OK, that was benchmarking
and profiling.

00:44:19.012 --> 00:44:20.720
Now you have all the
tools you need to be

00:44:20.720 --> 00:44:23.360
able to do performance things.

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:26.330
And now, we're going to write
some kernels in the remaining

00:44:26.330 --> 00:44:26.850
time.

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:29.430
So remember kernel fusion.

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:32.860
So this was the image that
I showed you in lecture.

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:35.500
There's a little factory, every
time I need to do an operation,

00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:37.000
I need to ship it
from the warehouse

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:38.480
to the factory and back.

00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:43.120
And so if I naively do a bunch
of operations in sequence

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:44.920
without thinking
about it, I'm paying

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:48.590
for a lot of shipping costs back
and forth from the warehouse.

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:50.890
What I should do is
have one factory that

00:44:50.890 --> 00:44:53.980
does all the operations at once
so I do not pay for this cost

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:54.830
multiple times.

00:44:54.830 --> 00:44:56.440
That's very important.

00:44:56.440 --> 00:44:58.780
So now we're going
to do gelu, and we're

00:44:58.780 --> 00:45:00.243
going to write a
kernel for gelu.

00:45:00.243 --> 00:45:01.660
And I'm going to
write that kernel

00:45:01.660 --> 00:45:03.430
in several different
ways, and we're

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:07.360
going to look at the performance
impact of doing that.

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:11.240
And so we have the PyTorch
implementation of gelu,

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:13.090
and that looks just like this.

00:45:13.090 --> 00:45:17.800
torch.nn functional gelu, and I
invoke approximate equals tanh,

00:45:17.800 --> 00:45:22.120
because I want this to exactly
match the naive thing that I'm

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:22.970
going to do next.

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:25.450
So this is not going to
be actually multiplying

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:28.773
by the CDF of the
Gaussian, it's going

00:45:28.773 --> 00:45:31.190
to be some approximation to
that that's easier to compute.

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:32.950
So that's the PyTorch gelu.

00:45:32.950 --> 00:45:34.860
And now, I'm going
to do the dumb thing.

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:36.610
You're going to look
at this code and say,

00:45:36.610 --> 00:45:38.920
this is going to
be low performance.

00:45:38.920 --> 00:45:41.290
I'm going to go in,
and in PyTorch, I'm

00:45:41.290 --> 00:45:43.870
going to write
gelu as 0.5 times x

00:45:43.870 --> 00:45:51.160
times 1 plus tanh h, square root
pi over 2 times x plus 0.044715

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:52.932
times x cubed.

00:45:52.932 --> 00:45:56.390
Magic formula, but this is a
good approximation to the gelu.

00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:59.800
You can look it up or convince
yourself this is true.

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:02.740
But if you do this,
you see that there's

00:46:02.740 --> 00:46:04.360
a lot of operations that happen.

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:06.830
There's a tanh,
there's a x cubed,

00:46:06.830 --> 00:46:08.530
there's multiplication
by a constant,

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:12.250
and addition and
multiplication by 0.5 and x.

00:46:12.250 --> 00:46:16.210
If this involves multiple
different CUDA kernels,

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:18.130
this is probably
going to be slow.

00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:20.800
That should be our intuition
at this point, from Fusion.

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:22.100
So let's see if that's true.

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:23.390
So these two are the same.

00:46:23.390 --> 00:46:26.530
You can see at the top left they
compute the exact same numbers.

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:31.100
And we can systematically
check this on random Gaussians.

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:33.180
And now, let's
benchmark the two.

00:46:33.180 --> 00:46:37.160
So the manual time
is 8.1 seconds

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:39.290
for a really, really big value.

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:43.340
And PyTorch time is 1.1
milliseconds, sorry.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:46.580
And the fused version is going
to be significantly faster,

00:46:46.580 --> 00:46:48.330
in fact, eight times faster.

00:46:48.330 --> 00:46:49.040
Wow.

00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:51.560
Big difference from
writing a simple kernel.

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:53.960
Of course, you're matmuls
are probably still going

00:46:53.960 --> 00:46:56.630
to be the bottleneck, but
it would be really cool

00:46:56.630 --> 00:46:58.520
if we could go from
that 8 milliseconds

00:46:58.520 --> 00:46:59.537
to that 1 millisecond.

00:46:59.537 --> 00:47:00.870
That would feel very satisfying.

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:04.130
So we're going to try to get
close to that 1.1 millisecond

00:47:04.130 --> 00:47:06.410
in the next few
parts of the lecture.

00:47:06.410 --> 00:47:10.102
So now let's look at what's
happening under the hood.

00:47:10.102 --> 00:47:11.810
I don't need to look
at NSIS, because all

00:47:11.810 --> 00:47:14.030
I really want to know is
some very high level stuff.

00:47:14.030 --> 00:47:17.055
For the manual gelu,
kind of just like I said,

00:47:17.055 --> 00:47:18.930
it's going to do a whole
bunch of operations.

00:47:18.930 --> 00:47:21.180
It's going to do a bunch
of multiplications.

00:47:21.180 --> 00:47:23.780
It's vectorized, but it's a
bunch of CUDA kernels being

00:47:23.780 --> 00:47:25.220
launched here.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:27.140
And notice on the
right, this CUDA kernel

00:47:27.140 --> 00:47:28.640
gets called three
times because we

00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:30.848
have a whole bunch of
multiplications floating around

00:47:30.848 --> 00:47:31.520
here.

00:47:31.520 --> 00:47:33.450
We've also got addition.

00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:34.820
We've got a tanh.

00:47:34.820 --> 00:47:37.230
And each one of these is
probably kind of slow.

00:47:37.230 --> 00:47:40.190
And in the end, we're
incurring fairly large overhead

00:47:40.190 --> 00:47:42.050
doing this.

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:47.160
Now, let's do the same
thing with the PyTorch gelu.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:48.810
And this is really great.

00:47:48.810 --> 00:47:50.790
There's a single
CUDA kernel launch.

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:53.550
It happens once and it just
processes the whole thing.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:55.400
This is what we'd like to see.

00:47:55.400 --> 00:47:57.260
And of course, this
is very, very fast,

00:47:57.260 --> 00:48:00.620
because it's just a
single CUDA kernel.

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:05.510
So this is really nice, and
we would like to somehow get

00:48:05.510 --> 00:48:06.810
to the CUDA kernel.

00:48:06.810 --> 00:48:08.690
And so the first thing
you might think of,

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:11.210
depending on how much
you know about writing

00:48:11.210 --> 00:48:15.110
GPU efficient code, is, all
right, the PyTorch people

00:48:15.110 --> 00:48:17.652
must have written this in the
lowest level language possible,

00:48:17.652 --> 00:48:19.152
so we're going to
do the same thing.

00:48:19.152 --> 00:48:21.420
We're going to go to-- not
the lowest level possible,

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:23.970
but we're going to
go to the C+ API,

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:27.470
and we're going to write
the CUDA kernel in C++.

00:48:27.470 --> 00:48:31.570
So let's open it up and
write our own CUDA kernel.

00:48:31.570 --> 00:48:32.990
So how is that going to work?

00:48:32.990 --> 00:48:38.810
So we have gone in and created a
C++ version of the whole thing.

00:48:38.810 --> 00:48:40.960
So CUDA, when we say
CUDA, is actually

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:45.470
the C API for interfacing
with and programming GPUs.

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:49.300
And just the logical
model of a GPU

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:51.910
that we described, we're going
to write some of them function,

00:48:51.910 --> 00:48:52.870
f.

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:56.060
And then when we invoke
this CUDA kernel,

00:48:56.060 --> 00:48:58.720
it's going to automatically
call f on all of the elements

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:01.270
of a vector, or a
matrix, and then we

00:49:01.270 --> 00:49:05.290
will get to parallel compute
everything that we want.

00:49:05.290 --> 00:49:08.050
As nomenclature, we're going
to have a grid, which is

00:49:08.050 --> 00:49:09.440
a collection of thread blocks.

00:49:09.440 --> 00:49:11.330
So think of this
as, I have a task,

00:49:11.330 --> 00:49:13.330
I'm going to cut
it up into pieces.

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:16.110
And there's going to
be a number of blocks.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:19.570
In a 2D grid, for example,
there's going to be a row

00:49:19.570 --> 00:49:22.070
coordinate and then there's
going to be a column coordinate.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.320
And this will be very useful if
you're working with matrices.

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:28.210
And then there will be the
size of each of these blocks.

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:32.290
How big are these in terms of
the number of thread blocks?

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:34.200
So this is the
dimension of the blocks.

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:37.725
And then there's a collection
of threads within these blocks.

00:49:37.725 --> 00:49:39.600
And this is the coordinate
that, for example,

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:42.180
one thread block lives
in, and then each thread

00:49:42.180 --> 00:49:43.630
is within each block.

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:45.520
So there's hierarchical
structure here.

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:48.040
There's a grid and then
there's a thread inside a grid.

00:49:48.040 --> 00:49:50.970
And then we're going
to, basically--

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:53.463
each function is going
to take in three things.

00:49:53.463 --> 00:49:55.380
It's going to take the
block index, like which

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:59.640
thread block do I belong
to, what side of the block

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:03.630
dimensions, and then what is
the index that [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:03.630 --> 00:50:05.290
like my thread index.

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:07.980
And with these, I can
know which coordinate

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:10.270
that I am in the
matrix or the vector,

00:50:10.270 --> 00:50:14.100
and then I can decide
what logic that I want.

00:50:14.100 --> 00:50:18.750
One last thing before we go
through the actual C++ code is

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:22.230
whenever you're
trying to debug CUDA,

00:50:22.230 --> 00:50:25.210
you want to launch with CUDA
launch blocking equals 1,

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:28.150
this will allow you to actually
debug your CUDA kernel.

00:50:28.150 --> 00:50:31.390
It will give you error
messages back at a cost,

00:50:31.390 --> 00:50:32.980
in terms of the runtime.

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:34.890
If you don't do
that, you are going

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:37.380
to have a bad time if
you're writing CUDA code

00:50:37.380 --> 00:50:38.910
and needing to debug.

00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:43.410
OK, here is my gelu code,
and let's go through it

00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:45.690
kind of piece by
piece, and then I'll

00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.060
talk about what all
the pieces are doing.

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:50.585
This will probably take the
longest out of the things

00:50:50.585 --> 00:50:51.960
that we're going
to walk through,

00:50:51.960 --> 00:50:53.910
other than the machine code.

00:50:53.910 --> 00:50:55.587
And once you
understand this, you

00:50:55.587 --> 00:50:57.670
should be able to understand
all the other pieces.

00:50:57.670 --> 00:51:00.000
So we'll go through
this a little slowly.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:01.870
So there's two
parts to this code.

00:51:01.870 --> 00:51:05.260
So the first part, this
gelu kernel piece up here,

00:51:05.260 --> 00:51:07.330
this is the actual kernel.

00:51:07.330 --> 00:51:08.830
This does the computation.

00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:10.660
This is going to
get sent to the GPU,

00:51:10.660 --> 00:51:12.250
it's going to do
the computation,

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:14.200
and then it will
return the results.

00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:18.220
This piece, the gelu function
here, this is a wrapper.

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:19.960
This lives on the
CPU, it's going

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:22.960
to orchestrate the launch of the
kernel, which is actually going

00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:25.330
to go out and live in the GPU.

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:29.300
So maybe we can start
with this wrapper piece,

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:30.680
this gelu function first.

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:33.670
So we're always going
to check two things.

00:51:33.670 --> 00:51:37.220
Basically in the Triton
or the CUDA code,

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:38.510
we're always going to check--

00:51:38.510 --> 00:51:39.080
oh, sorry.

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:40.010
There's a question back there.

00:51:40.010 --> 00:51:41.020
Sorry, I can't see it.

00:51:41.020 --> 00:51:41.535
Oh, OK.

00:51:41.535 --> 00:51:42.410
Sorry, that's my bad.

00:51:42.410 --> 00:51:43.720
OK, let me zoom in.

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:46.420
That is an easy
fix, but I needed

00:51:46.420 --> 00:51:47.630
to know that you can't see.

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:49.170
OK, good.

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:49.670
All right.

00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:50.390
Is this good?

00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:52.390
OK, excellent.

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:53.800
OK.

00:51:53.800 --> 00:51:55.763
So we're going to start
with the gelu function,

00:51:55.763 --> 00:51:57.430
and there's two things
that we're always

00:51:57.430 --> 00:51:58.263
going to need to do.

00:51:58.263 --> 00:52:04.240
The first one is to make sure
that x lives in the GPU device,

00:52:04.240 --> 00:52:06.140
like a CUDA tensor of some kind.

00:52:06.140 --> 00:52:10.082
If it's not, well, that's
going to be a problem.

00:52:10.082 --> 00:52:12.290
We're not going to be able
to do anything on the GPU.

00:52:12.290 --> 00:52:15.170
The second thing, which
is maybe less obvious,

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:17.890
is that we want to check to
make sure x is contiguous.

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:21.130
What that means is that it lives
in a contiguous block of memory,

00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:23.160
because when we
index into x, we're

00:52:23.160 --> 00:52:25.240
going to do a whole bunch
of indexing arithmetic,

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:28.150
and we're going to assume that
x lives in a block of memory.

00:52:28.150 --> 00:52:30.330
And if it doesn't,
it's just going

00:52:30.330 --> 00:52:32.340
to be basically
impossible to do this

00:52:32.340 --> 00:52:34.920
with any level of generality.

00:52:34.920 --> 00:52:38.820
And so when we compute the
gelu, we take in an input, x,

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:40.800
and we're going to output a y.

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:43.480
And so we need to
allocate an output.

00:52:43.480 --> 00:52:46.930
So torch::Tensor y equals
torch::empty_like(x).

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:50.910
This is just saying, well,
give me an output tensor

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:53.670
space or a pointer
to an output tensor

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:56.500
that is just like
the dimension of x.

00:52:56.500 --> 00:52:58.840
And notice that I'm
not calling zeros.

00:52:58.840 --> 00:53:01.090
This will save on
extra operations.

00:53:01.090 --> 00:53:02.340
I don't need to 0 out these.

00:53:02.340 --> 00:53:02.730
Why?

00:53:02.730 --> 00:53:04.563
Because I'm going to
write into them anyway.

00:53:04.563 --> 00:53:07.890
So this is a minor, but
you might as well do it,

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:08.860
optimization.

00:53:08.860 --> 00:53:12.210
And then, basically, in
all the code that we write,

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:14.595
we're going to need to
figure out the grid.

00:53:14.595 --> 00:53:17.310
So what's the total number
of elements that I have?

00:53:17.310 --> 00:53:19.380
What's the size of each block?

00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:21.590
The number of threads
that I have in each block?

00:53:21.590 --> 00:53:24.330
And then, how many
blocks total do I have?

00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:27.060
And when I need to figure
out the number of blocks,

00:53:27.060 --> 00:53:30.110
I'm going to call cdiv,
which is going to be,

00:53:30.110 --> 00:53:33.200
essentially, take the ratio
of num elements to blocksize

00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:34.680
and then take the ceiling.

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:37.310
Because I need to
round up to make sure

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:39.530
that very last set
of elements that

00:53:39.530 --> 00:53:42.320
isn't divisible by blocksize
still gets computed.

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:45.440
So I take the ceiling
rather than the floor.

00:53:45.440 --> 00:53:48.315
And then this is all very
simple bookkeeping stuff.

00:53:48.315 --> 00:53:50.190
And then I say, all
right, launch the kernel.

00:53:50.190 --> 00:53:52.550
And the gelu kernel
gets launched.

00:53:52.550 --> 00:53:56.360
And this angle brackets is
saying, this is kind of the--

00:53:56.360 --> 00:53:59.550
the given number of blocks,
and the size of each block.

00:53:59.550 --> 00:54:03.450
And this is going to be passed
into the kernel command.

00:54:03.450 --> 00:54:06.150
And then I'm going to pass in
the pointers to x's and y's.

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:08.480
I'm not actually going
to pass the values

00:54:08.480 --> 00:54:11.460
of x's and y's, and the
total number of elements.

00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:14.740
And I need this to compute,
essentially, the boundary

00:54:14.740 --> 00:54:17.230
conditions of my kernel.

00:54:17.230 --> 00:54:20.300
So now let's go to the
actual kernel itself.

00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:23.420
So I have global
void gelukernel,

00:54:23.420 --> 00:54:25.070
and I get in pointers
for in and out,

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:27.310
and I have number
of elements items.

00:54:27.310 --> 00:54:30.820
And this keyword
global, the website--

00:54:30.820 --> 00:54:33.680
sorry, the rendering here
has mangled it a little bit,

00:54:33.680 --> 00:54:35.990
but you should think
of this as __global.

00:54:35.990 --> 00:54:37.960
And this is a keyword
that distinguishes it

00:54:37.960 --> 00:54:41.200
as a CUDA kernel function.

00:54:41.200 --> 00:54:42.470
And so what am I doing?

00:54:42.470 --> 00:54:45.970
Well, this thread
is actually supposed

00:54:45.970 --> 00:54:48.970
to operate on a single
element, i, right?

00:54:48.970 --> 00:54:51.293
But I don't get i as input.

00:54:51.293 --> 00:54:52.960
Like, the code doesn't
actually tell me,

00:54:52.960 --> 00:54:55.040
you're in a vector
in coordinate i.

00:54:55.040 --> 00:54:56.980
So I need to compute where I am.

00:54:56.980 --> 00:54:59.410
And how am I going to
do that, it's going

00:54:59.410 --> 00:55:01.070
to be I take my block index.

00:55:01.070 --> 00:55:04.360
I only have one dimension, so
it's block index.x, so just

00:55:04.360 --> 00:55:05.950
the first coordinate.

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:10.450
And then multiply it by the size
of each block, the blockDim.x.

00:55:10.450 --> 00:55:12.780
And this tells me,
basically, the starting point

00:55:12.780 --> 00:55:14.920
within my current block.

00:55:14.920 --> 00:55:17.550
And then now I add
in threadIdx so I

00:55:17.550 --> 00:55:19.630
know where the start
of my current block is,

00:55:19.630 --> 00:55:22.720
and I add in the offset to
where I am within the block,

00:55:22.720 --> 00:55:25.630
and that gives me my
global coordinate i.

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:27.690
So some bookkeeping
computation just

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:29.460
to get the coordinates here.

00:55:29.460 --> 00:55:31.410
And then this is important, too.

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:34.080
You see this pattern
basically in all the CUDA code

00:55:34.080 --> 00:55:35.550
that people write.

00:55:35.550 --> 00:55:38.470
There's no kind of out of
bounds checking, naturally.

00:55:38.470 --> 00:55:40.500
And so what you do is
I have my coordinate

00:55:40.500 --> 00:55:42.330
and I'm going to
check to make sure

00:55:42.330 --> 00:55:45.090
that I am supposed to be
processing something that's

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:45.850
in bounds.

00:55:45.850 --> 00:55:48.188
And some of the threads at
the very end of your block,

00:55:48.188 --> 00:55:49.980
they're going to be
processing stuff that's

00:55:49.980 --> 00:55:53.140
out of bounds in memory, and you
do not want it to touch those,

00:55:53.140 --> 00:55:55.950
and so you basically condition
it on i less than num elements,

00:55:55.950 --> 00:55:58.030
and you do nothing if
you're outside of that.

00:55:58.030 --> 00:55:58.535
Sorry, yes.

00:55:58.535 --> 00:56:01.260
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:03.150 --> 00:56:06.000
Sorry, this is
just the extension

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:08.140
that you write the CUDA code in.

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:12.960
It's to distinguish it from
just your standard C code.

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:16.030
OK, so it's just
a file name thing.

00:56:16.030 --> 00:56:16.840
It's just .cu.

00:56:16.840 --> 00:56:19.230
There's nothing particularly
special about it.

00:56:19.230 --> 00:56:21.660
OK, and then so
now, within here,

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:23.620
we're going to just
do our computation.

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:25.590
It's just going to be
I'm going to write out.

00:56:25.590 --> 00:56:29.260
I have my input, in, I'm going
to index into the ith element,

00:56:29.260 --> 00:56:32.200
and I compute my gelu
just like I did before,

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:35.942
and I assign it to out
of i, and then I'm done.

00:56:35.942 --> 00:56:37.150
That's all that I need to do.

00:56:37.150 --> 00:56:38.850
And since this is
all pointer stuff,

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:40.650
I don't really need
to worry too much

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:43.740
about what is kind of
actually happening here.

00:56:43.740 --> 00:56:46.450
So that's basically it.

00:56:46.450 --> 00:56:50.830
I can then take my CUDA
gelu code that I have,

00:56:50.830 --> 00:56:53.950
and then I can load
this C++ code in line,

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:57.060
and then I can just have it
compile into a module all within

00:56:57.060 --> 00:56:57.723
Python.

00:56:57.723 --> 00:56:59.140
It's all very nice
and convenient.

00:56:59.140 --> 00:57:01.950
You don't really have to go
out onto the command line

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:02.680
and do things.

00:57:02.680 --> 00:57:05.910
And so now, we
have CUDA defined.

00:57:05.910 --> 00:57:07.210
So this is nice.

00:57:07.210 --> 00:57:10.630
And basically, it's a
compilation of this,

00:57:10.630 --> 00:57:12.980
and I can call it
from within Python.

00:57:12.980 --> 00:57:16.840
And we'll use the C
bindings to call this guy.

00:57:16.840 --> 00:57:19.140
OK, we're done
calling CUDA gelu.

00:57:21.706 --> 00:57:24.970
I can check that the manual
gelu and the CUDA gelu

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:27.460
are the same, and now
let's benchmark the two.

00:57:27.460 --> 00:57:30.170
So I have the time that
it takes to run PyTorch.

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:33.580
And just like last time,
it's about 1.1 milliseconds.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:36.950
And manual time, remember,
it's 8.1 milliseconds,

00:57:36.950 --> 00:57:37.870
and so drumroll.

00:57:37.870 --> 00:57:39.620
What is our CUDA time?

00:57:39.620 --> 00:57:41.320
Well, we've gotten
it down to 1.8.

00:57:41.320 --> 00:57:44.090
Not quite as good as
PyTorch's implementation,

00:57:44.090 --> 00:57:47.800
but we're getting pretty
close to PyTorch time.

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:51.140
We've gone from 8 milliseconds
to 1.8 milliseconds,

00:57:51.140 --> 00:57:54.680
which is not bad, because that C
code wasn't that hard to write.

00:57:54.680 --> 00:57:57.910
And so now we also
do some profiling.

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:01.060
And we can see what
is happening here now.

00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:03.110
And it's called the gelu kernel.

00:58:03.110 --> 00:58:07.020
This is the code that got
shipped off to the GPU.

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:09.040
And then it's
calling empty_like.

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:11.350
This is the initialization.

00:58:11.350 --> 00:58:13.800
And then empty_strided.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:17.490
And then CUDA launch kernel
and CUDA device synchronize.

00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:19.540
And that's basically
all that's happening.

00:58:19.540 --> 00:58:22.350
And notice how, once again,
this is a single CUDA kernel

00:58:22.350 --> 00:58:26.070
eats up 100% of the GPU
time, kind of what we wanted.

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:28.510
So there's some further
optimization we can do,

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:30.460
but this is really
already solved

00:58:30.460 --> 00:58:32.590
the problem of kernel fusion.

00:58:32.590 --> 00:58:36.140
We fused all the
operators together.

00:58:36.140 --> 00:58:37.890
So pretty good.

00:58:37.890 --> 00:58:39.600
These kinds of
element-wise operations

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:40.810
are easy to write in CUDA.

00:58:40.810 --> 00:58:42.180
If you have a new kind of--

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:45.780
I don't know, nonlinearity,
you could easily

00:58:45.780 --> 00:58:47.580
write a CUDA kernel
for it yourself,

00:58:47.580 --> 00:58:49.110
if you really wanted to.

00:58:49.110 --> 00:58:50.820
But more interesting
operations are

00:58:50.820 --> 00:58:52.750
going to require
reading multiple values,

00:58:52.750 --> 00:58:53.798
like doing reductions.

00:58:53.798 --> 00:58:55.840
Those are going to get a
little more complicated.

00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:59.250
Flash attention will be a little
bit more complicated, but not

00:58:59.250 --> 00:59:03.462
too much so when you have
to do it in the assignment.

00:59:03.462 --> 00:59:08.595
And questions on the
simple C++ CUDA kernel?

00:59:08.595 --> 00:59:09.095
Yes.

00:59:11.273 --> 00:59:12.690
So another check
at the beginning.

00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:13.663
Yeah.

00:59:13.663 --> 00:59:15.080
Is that [INAUDIBLE]
error or is it

00:59:15.080 --> 00:59:17.030
some slower kernel or something?

00:59:17.030 --> 00:59:19.620
Yeah, so the question was what
happens if it's not contiguous?

00:59:19.620 --> 00:59:21.300
At least in the
code that we wrote,

00:59:21.300 --> 00:59:23.900
it will just throw an error,
because it's an assert.

00:59:23.900 --> 00:59:26.310
You could potentially
write code to handle it,

00:59:26.310 --> 00:59:29.660
but there's almost no reason
for memory to be fragmented,

00:59:29.660 --> 00:59:32.420
because it will
allocate contiguously.

00:59:32.420 --> 00:59:34.910
And you won't deallocate
the middle of a memory,

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:37.490
unless you're doing
something really tricky.

00:59:37.490 --> 00:59:39.200
And so you should
really-- unless you're

00:59:39.200 --> 00:59:41.460
doing something
pretty advanced--

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:44.660
expect to have
contiguous memory.

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:47.600
[INAUDIBLE] do a transpose
or some operation that

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:49.177
makes memory non-contiguous?

00:59:49.177 --> 00:59:49.677
Right.

00:59:49.677 --> 00:59:53.870
So when you're coding at a
higher level, be careful that,

00:59:53.870 --> 00:59:55.370
conversely, you may--

00:59:55.370 --> 00:59:58.400
like you forced [INAUDIBLE]
on operations that implement.

00:59:58.400 --> 01:00:02.085
Yeah, so the question was
like, if you're transposing,

01:00:02.085 --> 01:00:03.960
then you're no longer
going to be contiguous,

01:00:03.960 --> 01:00:05.757
you're going to have
a jump between all

01:00:05.757 --> 01:00:06.840
the elements in the index.

01:00:06.840 --> 01:00:10.340
If you're traversing something,
that's column stored.

01:00:10.340 --> 01:00:14.120
Yeah, so I think transpose or
views or essentially shuffling

01:00:14.120 --> 01:00:16.170
dimension is the one
exception to this,

01:00:16.170 --> 01:00:19.680
but that's handleable in
the outer the wrapper part.

01:00:19.680 --> 01:00:21.740
You can basically
pass it something

01:00:21.740 --> 01:00:23.720
that is contiguously indexed.

01:00:23.720 --> 01:00:26.520
And for a lot of the matrices,
you won't really care.

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:28.970
So yes?

01:00:28.970 --> 01:00:31.845
What would happen if you were
to choose a different blocksize?

01:00:31.845 --> 01:00:32.345
Right.

01:00:32.345 --> 01:00:35.900
So what would happen if you
chose a different blocksize?

01:00:35.900 --> 01:00:40.640
The GPU-related of
concerns would kick in.

01:00:40.640 --> 01:00:44.450
Do you have enough blocks
for it to saturate your SMs?

01:00:44.450 --> 01:00:48.030
And do you have enough
work within each block?

01:00:48.030 --> 01:00:51.030
And those are the two things
that could matter here.

01:00:51.030 --> 01:00:54.620
But I think-- my guess is
that for block sizes that

01:00:54.620 --> 01:00:57.713
are relatively large, like
1024, it probably won't

01:00:57.713 --> 01:00:58.880
matter past a certain point.

01:00:58.880 --> 01:01:00.120
Because we're not doing
anything advanced,

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:02.720
it's all entrywise operations
for this very, very simple

01:01:02.720 --> 01:01:03.620
example.

01:01:03.620 --> 01:01:04.610
Yeah.

01:01:04.610 --> 01:01:08.390
Is the reason that our
non-GP [INAUDIBLE] so slow

01:01:08.390 --> 01:01:12.320
because this queue was asked
to do some operation on the GPU

01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:16.040
and send it back [INAUDIBLE]
other small things [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:16.040 --> 01:01:20.270
So the question was why was
our non-CUDA kernel manual

01:01:20.270 --> 01:01:21.680
thing so slow?

01:01:21.680 --> 01:01:24.890
It's not that it's sending
things back from GPU to CPU,

01:01:24.890 --> 01:01:25.410
per se.

01:01:25.410 --> 01:01:27.360
Like, x is going
to live in the GPU.

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:29.240
We allocate it in GPU.

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:32.810
We'll do as device
like CUDA, but it's

01:01:32.810 --> 01:01:36.290
going to basically not be
in the SM the whole time.

01:01:36.290 --> 01:01:40.650
So once we do x squared,
that's a CUDA kernel.

01:01:40.650 --> 01:01:44.480
And so that multiplication
operation will read the vector

01:01:44.480 --> 01:01:47.610
from the global memory into
the SMs, do the computation,

01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:48.540
it'll write it back.

01:01:48.540 --> 01:01:52.160
And so this is all
in the DRAM to SM

01:01:52.160 --> 01:01:54.590
communication cost
rather than the CPU

01:01:54.590 --> 01:01:56.570
to GPU communication cost.

01:01:56.570 --> 01:01:58.870
Of course, if you write
like, as device CPU,

01:01:58.870 --> 01:02:01.980
then you'll get the CPU
transfer cost in addition

01:02:01.980 --> 01:02:05.910
to the DRAM transfer cost.

01:02:05.910 --> 01:02:11.020
OK, so now you've seen that in--
so that was not too painful.

01:02:11.020 --> 01:02:15.660
But it would be really nice if
we had nicer Python abstractions

01:02:15.660 --> 01:02:17.260
for writing CUDA kernels.

01:02:17.260 --> 01:02:18.940
And this is what Triton is.

01:02:18.940 --> 01:02:20.530
And Triton is quite nice.

01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:22.723
It has this very
nice middle ground

01:02:22.723 --> 01:02:24.390
where you don't have
to manage literally

01:02:24.390 --> 01:02:25.990
everything about the GPU.

01:02:25.990 --> 01:02:32.550
So Triton is a domain-specific
language developed by OpenAI

01:02:32.550 --> 01:02:36.810
in 2021, and it makes
GPU programming much more

01:02:36.810 --> 01:02:37.480
accessible.

01:02:37.480 --> 01:02:40.860
So you write everything
kind of in Python,

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:43.900
and you don't really think
about the threads anymore.

01:02:43.900 --> 01:02:46.080
You think about thread blocks.

01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:50.050
And Triton manages a lot
of stuff that is annoying,

01:02:50.050 --> 01:02:52.020
but can be
automatically optimized.

01:02:52.020 --> 01:02:56.090
So it can manage
coalescing of memory.

01:02:56.090 --> 01:03:01.280
So remember that from DRAM
you get four adjacent values

01:03:01.280 --> 01:03:03.660
at once, what's something
called burst mode.

01:03:03.660 --> 01:03:07.280
So you really want to make sure
that your memory retrievals are

01:03:07.280 --> 01:03:13.537
of grouped into adjacent four
elements or more calls at once.

01:03:13.537 --> 01:03:15.120
So it will handle
those automatically.

01:03:15.120 --> 01:03:16.760
It will group those.

01:03:16.760 --> 01:03:18.980
It will do shared
memory management

01:03:18.980 --> 01:03:24.440
when you need to manage which
memory that you're writing to

01:03:24.440 --> 01:03:27.650
within the SM, with
multiple threads.

01:03:27.650 --> 01:03:31.250
From within each SM, you might
need to stop or start threads

01:03:31.250 --> 01:03:34.850
all managed automatically,
but scheduling across SMs

01:03:34.850 --> 01:03:37.410
or what different SMs
do, that's manual.

01:03:37.410 --> 01:03:39.440
So the programming
model is that you're

01:03:39.440 --> 01:03:41.970
going to think kind of
at the SM-centric level,

01:03:41.970 --> 01:03:44.870
and the compiler will handle
a lot more of the lower level

01:03:44.870 --> 01:03:46.310
details.

01:03:46.310 --> 01:03:48.890
And Triton is quite
nice because it

01:03:48.890 --> 01:03:52.500
can outperform, by quite a bit,
a lot PyTorch implementations.

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:55.280
So it's kind of going all
the way to writing CUDA,

01:03:55.280 --> 01:03:58.190
but you're still in the
very familiar Python land.

01:03:58.190 --> 01:04:01.030
And I think a very
underappreciated advantage

01:04:01.030 --> 01:04:04.160
is, as it's written
here, it's all in Python.

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:07.910
You can step through it, you
can debug it fairly nicely.

01:04:07.910 --> 01:04:10.490
And so let's step
through a trying kernel.

01:04:10.490 --> 01:04:12.850
Once again, we're
going to write gelu,

01:04:12.850 --> 01:04:15.500
and we're going to
do it in Triton.

01:04:15.500 --> 01:04:20.350
So this, I've put the code to be
as similar structure as possible

01:04:20.350 --> 01:04:21.320
to our other code.

01:04:21.320 --> 01:04:24.560
So this is of the CPU
side code, so to speak.

01:04:24.560 --> 01:04:26.810
This is the wrapper
Triton gelu code.

01:04:26.810 --> 01:04:28.910
It takes in x, which
is a Torch tensor,

01:04:28.910 --> 01:04:31.240
and I've got my two
asserts at the top.

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:34.450
And I'm going to
allocate an output tensor

01:04:34.450 --> 01:04:36.710
y using empty_like, once again.

01:04:36.710 --> 01:04:40.180
And it has the same exact
coordinate computation

01:04:40.180 --> 01:04:41.980
components.

01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:44.480
And even the kernel
launch looks very similar.

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:47.030
I've got this
num_blocks annotation,

01:04:47.030 --> 01:04:49.790
and then my blocksize
is at the end here,

01:04:49.790 --> 01:04:51.990
not in part of this
brackets, but basically

01:04:51.990 --> 01:04:54.790
in passing the same
information to my kernel.

01:04:54.790 --> 01:04:59.760
And now triangle kernel
is this code over here.

01:04:59.760 --> 01:05:01.920
And this is going
to do the same thing

01:05:01.920 --> 01:05:04.290
as what we were doing
before, but now it's

01:05:04.290 --> 01:05:06.420
nicely written in Python.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:09.510
And the mental model
here is the inputs

01:05:09.510 --> 01:05:11.580
are going to be at x pointer.

01:05:11.580 --> 01:05:15.400
Y pointer is the output vector,
the starting coordinate.

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:18.970
And the blocksize is how
big each of my blocks are.

01:05:18.970 --> 01:05:22.320
And num_elements is going to
be the very end of my array.

01:05:22.320 --> 01:05:28.360
So now I need to get this
set of lines, 557 7 to 561,

01:05:28.360 --> 01:05:31.800
this is doing the
computation of my index.

01:05:31.800 --> 01:05:34.545
I did i equals some
formula before,

01:05:34.545 --> 01:05:36.420
this is doing the same
calculation over here.

01:05:36.420 --> 01:05:39.120
I'm calculating where is the
start of my current block, well,

01:05:39.120 --> 01:05:42.120
that's my block ID, times
the size of the block, that

01:05:42.120 --> 01:05:43.810
gets me-- let's say
I live in block one,

01:05:43.810 --> 01:05:46.950
it'll get me this point
right here at the middle.

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:49.790
And then afterwards,
I need to know

01:05:49.790 --> 01:05:52.320
where do I live
within my block, well,

01:05:52.320 --> 01:05:54.420
that's going to be the offset.

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:56.420
But now, notice one difference.

01:05:56.420 --> 01:05:59.780
I don't get in an offset because
I'm not programming threads.

01:05:59.780 --> 01:06:01.680
I'm programming blocks.

01:06:01.680 --> 01:06:03.060
And so what does that mean?

01:06:03.060 --> 01:06:07.245
Well, my offsets are actually
a vector, not a single value.

01:06:07.245 --> 01:06:08.870
Because this is
basically going to be--

01:06:08.870 --> 01:06:11.330
I'm going to do vectorized
operation, where

01:06:11.330 --> 01:06:13.550
the vectorized
operation is going to be

01:06:13.550 --> 01:06:15.470
handled by different threads.

01:06:15.470 --> 01:06:17.870
So here, my offsets
are the start

01:06:17.870 --> 01:06:23.220
of the block plus a vector,
this range of blocksize offset.

01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:26.870
So I'm my offsets are all of
these coordinates within block

01:06:26.870 --> 01:06:28.200
one at once.

01:06:28.200 --> 01:06:31.140
Of course, if I'm at the very
end, I might go off the edge

01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:34.280
and so I need a mask to
handle anything that lives off

01:06:34.280 --> 01:06:37.250
the boundary of my vector.

01:06:37.250 --> 01:06:42.020
Now, I'm going to load in a
single vectorized operation,

01:06:42.020 --> 01:06:44.580
everything at once.

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:47.660
So x pointer plus offsets,
these are the values

01:06:47.660 --> 01:06:49.850
that I'm responsible
for, masked up,

01:06:49.850 --> 01:06:55.670
and it's loaded into x,
which is my internal values,

01:06:55.670 --> 01:06:58.670
my internal temporary
vector that I need.

01:06:58.670 --> 01:07:00.160
And with this
temporary vector, I'm

01:07:00.160 --> 01:07:03.760
going to do exactly the
old gelu computation.

01:07:03.760 --> 01:07:06.020
There's no tanh, so I
compute that manually.

01:07:06.020 --> 01:07:07.940
But this formula, you
can convince yourself,

01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:10.840
is the same as
what we have here.

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:14.750
And then y is going to be
the formula computed up here.

01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:17.230
Now once I'm done, I
need to write it back

01:07:17.230 --> 01:07:21.080
into my output buffer,
or my output vector.

01:07:21.080 --> 01:07:23.240
And so I compute my targets.

01:07:23.240 --> 01:07:24.790
So this is y pointer
plus offsets.

01:07:24.790 --> 01:07:29.520
I take my values, my temporary
values, y, and then I store it.

01:07:29.520 --> 01:07:33.020
So this is very, very, very
similar to what came before,

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:34.850
but this one is the
vectorized version.

01:07:34.850 --> 01:07:37.100
I get to operate on an
entire block at once.

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:40.760
And so instead of thinking at
the perspective of a thread,

01:07:40.760 --> 01:07:42.830
I'm thinking from the
perspective of a block,

01:07:42.830 --> 01:07:44.560
but not too different.

01:07:44.560 --> 01:07:47.590
This is all fairly
similar stuff.

01:07:47.590 --> 01:07:50.760
So now, I've written
my Triton gelu and--

01:07:50.760 --> 01:07:52.940
all right, I will do
this fairly quickly.

01:07:52.940 --> 01:07:53.440
All right.

01:07:53.440 --> 01:07:54.673
So one last thing.

01:07:54.673 --> 01:07:56.340
I will only point out
a few things here,

01:07:56.340 --> 01:07:59.640
because I don't want to get so
in the weeds that you all get up

01:07:59.640 --> 01:08:00.810
and leave.

01:08:00.810 --> 01:08:05.190
But the one last cool thing that
we can do is Triton, of course,

01:08:05.190 --> 01:08:10.180
compiles into low level, almost
machine code for the GPU.

01:08:10.180 --> 01:08:12.480
And we can look at
this very low level

01:08:12.480 --> 01:08:18.010
code called PTX code, after the
Triton compiler goes over it.

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:19.779
And it's actually cool.

01:08:19.779 --> 01:08:24.130
You can see how the GPU actually
works at the thread level.

01:08:24.130 --> 01:08:26.319
So this is the
Triton gelu kernel.

01:08:26.319 --> 01:08:28.380
It was generated
by the compiler.

01:08:28.380 --> 01:08:32.260
And at first, it's going to do
some of the really basic stuff.

01:08:32.260 --> 01:08:33.550
So what's it doing here?

01:08:33.550 --> 01:08:36.819
It's saying, well, I'm going
to need to store some values.

01:08:36.819 --> 01:08:39.250
I'm going to need to store
intermediate computations.

01:08:39.250 --> 01:08:41.670
b means, actually, untyped--

01:08:41.670 --> 01:08:43.029
basically, like bytes.

01:08:43.029 --> 01:08:46.800
So I need bites that
are of 32-bit size.

01:08:46.800 --> 01:08:50.130
I need floats for doing
computations, called f.

01:08:50.130 --> 01:08:52.399
And I need another
set of registers

01:08:52.399 --> 01:08:56.930
that are 64 bits, and that's
another set of registers.

01:08:56.930 --> 01:08:59.180
And so I have all
of these registers

01:08:59.180 --> 01:09:01.170
that I need for
temporary computations.

01:09:01.170 --> 01:09:04.399
And then starting here, I'm
going to start computing,

01:09:04.399 --> 01:09:05.890
basically, my coordinates.

01:09:05.890 --> 01:09:06.390
Oh, sorry.

01:09:06.390 --> 01:09:11.310
This part is loading the various
arguments to the function.

01:09:11.310 --> 01:09:15.500
So things like the x pointer and
the y pointer get loaded here.

01:09:15.500 --> 01:09:19.880
Starting here, I start
computing the coordinate offsets

01:09:19.880 --> 01:09:22.200
of my Triton kernel.

01:09:22.200 --> 01:09:26.010
And then once I get down
here, this LD global,

01:09:26.010 --> 01:09:29.240
this is the code
that's used to load

01:09:29.240 --> 01:09:33.779
the values from x pointer back
into my temporary registers.

01:09:33.779 --> 01:09:39.410
So it's basically saying
load r2, r3, r4, r5 using

01:09:39.410 --> 01:09:43.380
the memory position in rd1.

01:09:43.380 --> 01:09:45.880
And notice how it's loading
four things at once because it's

01:09:45.880 --> 01:09:47.470
cleverly handling coalescing.

01:09:47.470 --> 01:09:49.250
We know we can get
four values for free,

01:09:49.250 --> 01:09:51.790
we should operate on
all four of these values

01:09:51.790 --> 01:09:53.060
at once because we get them.

01:09:53.060 --> 01:09:58.720
And then you do the
same thing again for--

01:09:58.720 --> 01:10:00.380
you do the same
thing again here.

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:02.350
And then you start
to get, basically,

01:10:02.350 --> 01:10:05.090
the floating point
operations, mul.f32,

01:10:05.090 --> 01:10:08.240
which basically goes through
and does the tanh computations.

01:10:08.240 --> 01:10:10.580
I'm not going to explain
all the different pieces,

01:10:10.580 --> 01:10:13.640
but here it's multiplying
by a constant,

01:10:13.640 --> 01:10:17.260
it does a x to the cube by
multiplying the same numbers

01:10:17.260 --> 01:10:19.120
multiple times.

01:10:19.120 --> 01:10:23.240
And then it's going to
compute here, 2 to the x,

01:10:23.240 --> 01:10:26.950
but we want e to the x, and
so it multiplies by log 2

01:10:26.950 --> 01:10:29.060
to get the exponentiated base.

01:10:29.060 --> 01:10:32.530
And you can really see all
of the different literal

01:10:32.530 --> 01:10:35.380
step-by-step operations
that the GPU does in order

01:10:35.380 --> 01:10:37.220
to get you the final result.

01:10:37.220 --> 01:10:38.905
And so I'll skip
all over to the end.

01:10:38.905 --> 01:10:40.530
This is all floating
point computations

01:10:40.530 --> 01:10:41.950
that it needs to do.

01:10:41.950 --> 01:10:44.100
And then at the very
end, it stores the values

01:10:44.100 --> 01:10:49.050
that it has, r38 through r41,
into rd4, which is the memory

01:10:49.050 --> 01:10:50.660
position of our output.

01:10:50.660 --> 01:10:54.480
So this is what's actually
happening at the low level.

01:10:54.480 --> 01:10:56.790
And we see that each
thread is operating

01:10:56.790 --> 01:10:58.450
on four values at a time.

01:10:58.450 --> 01:11:00.610
And its temporary
storage is the registers,

01:11:00.610 --> 01:11:03.060
which is the really,
really high speed storage

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:04.180
that it has very locally.

01:11:04.180 --> 01:11:06.960
So we can see this is going
to, just looking at it,

01:11:06.960 --> 01:11:09.780
be probably pretty fast code.

01:11:09.780 --> 01:11:10.530
OK.

01:11:10.530 --> 01:11:13.800
So that was the PTX, and we can
go through and see what it's

01:11:13.800 --> 01:11:16.540
doing for all sorts of things.

01:11:16.540 --> 01:11:19.690
But now, let's go back and
actually benchmark things.

01:11:19.690 --> 01:11:22.320
So we got manual gelu,
8.1 seconds, PyTorch time,

01:11:22.320 --> 01:11:26.250
1.1 seconds, CUDA time,
1.84 seconds, Triton time,

01:11:26.250 --> 01:11:27.880
1.848 seconds.

01:11:27.880 --> 01:11:31.410
So we didn't get any faster,
but it was much easier

01:11:31.410 --> 01:11:32.460
to write Triton code.

01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:34.870
We wrote it in Python,
we thought about blocks,

01:11:34.870 --> 01:11:36.780
we could do
vectorized additions.

01:11:36.780 --> 01:11:39.340
If you're doing more
sophisticated stuff,

01:11:39.340 --> 01:11:43.530
basically, Triton will handle a
lot of the memory stuff for you.

01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:45.790
And so it's actually
pretty good.

01:11:45.790 --> 01:11:47.430
And then profiling,
once again, we

01:11:47.430 --> 01:11:52.622
see single kernel launch that
consumes all of the GPU time.

01:11:52.622 --> 01:11:57.030
So that's great, and
that gets Triton kernels.

01:11:57.030 --> 01:12:01.248
The last thing--
at least in this--

01:12:01.248 --> 01:12:03.930
whoops-- one second.

01:12:03.930 --> 01:12:08.250
Here, OK-- that I want to
talk about is torch.compile.

01:12:08.250 --> 01:12:10.320
Of course, writing
CUDA kernels is cool

01:12:10.320 --> 01:12:12.210
and it makes you
feel really good,

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:15.030
but maybe we don't
need to do that.

01:12:15.030 --> 01:12:17.530
The things that we were
doing here were very simple.

01:12:17.530 --> 01:12:21.210
We were just taking these
x cubed and exponentiation

01:12:21.210 --> 01:12:23.010
operations and we
were just shoving them

01:12:23.010 --> 01:12:25.600
all into a single CUDA kernel.

01:12:25.600 --> 01:12:29.620
And so maybe we can just
do that without doing much.

01:12:29.620 --> 01:12:32.280
And so we've had the
several different ways

01:12:32.280 --> 01:12:35.220
that we've showed you, but the
last one I want to talk about

01:12:35.220 --> 01:12:37.010
is this thing called
torch.compile,

01:12:37.010 --> 01:12:42.010
which will take a
non-optimized PyTorch code

01:12:42.010 --> 01:12:45.590
and it will write
more optimized code.

01:12:45.590 --> 01:12:47.140
And so here it's
going to attempt

01:12:47.140 --> 01:12:48.730
to automatically
do optimizations,

01:12:48.730 --> 01:12:51.250
like kernel fusion.

01:12:51.250 --> 01:12:54.100
And this compiled
gelu is going to be

01:12:54.100 --> 01:12:57.740
equivalent in the actual
outputs that it generates.

01:12:57.740 --> 01:13:00.580
But now let's look
at the runtimes.

01:13:00.580 --> 01:13:04.338
So we've got some runtime
variation, but basically

01:13:04.338 --> 01:13:05.380
the same kind of numbers.

01:13:05.380 --> 01:13:09.520
8.1 seconds, manual, 1.1
seconds, PyTorch, 1.8 seconds,

01:13:09.520 --> 01:13:15.760
CUDA, and then 1.47
seconds on torch.compile.

01:13:15.760 --> 01:13:19.850
So the punchline here is modern
JIT compilers are pretty good.

01:13:19.850 --> 01:13:22.750
It can do optimizations
like operation fusion,

01:13:22.750 --> 01:13:25.910
without you having to
do very much at all.

01:13:25.910 --> 01:13:28.750
And if you look
under the hood, you

01:13:28.750 --> 01:13:32.690
can see that there's,
basically, once again,

01:13:32.690 --> 01:13:34.370
one thing that happens.

01:13:34.370 --> 01:13:39.250
This is a fused add,
multiply, tanh Triton code.

01:13:39.250 --> 01:13:42.840
So it's generating Triton under
the hood, that basically is

01:13:42.840 --> 01:13:44.950
doing similar kinds of
things as our Triton code,

01:13:44.950 --> 01:13:48.202
but is actually slightly more
optimized than what we did.

01:13:48.202 --> 01:13:50.160
And so it's getting
slightly better performance

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:52.230
than even our code.

01:13:52.230 --> 01:13:54.870
So torch.compile is quite nice.

01:13:54.870 --> 01:13:55.920
Yes.

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:59.880
How do you [INAUDIBLE] that
torch.compile will do better?

01:13:59.880 --> 01:14:02.970
Do you just need to try to
implement your previous version

01:14:02.970 --> 01:14:04.560
and see if it works--

01:14:04.560 --> 01:14:07.260
like it can't do
[INAUDIBLE], right?

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:11.107
Yeah, so so the question was
like, when do you know that--

01:14:11.107 --> 01:14:12.690
I guess maybe the
better way to phrase

01:14:12.690 --> 01:14:14.190
that question is,
when do you can

01:14:14.190 --> 01:14:18.480
do better than torch.compile,
right, is the relevant question?

01:14:18.480 --> 01:14:22.320
And I think for simple stuff,
like simple operator fusion.

01:14:22.320 --> 01:14:24.540
Or the other thing
that it's very good at

01:14:24.540 --> 01:14:28.050
is optimizing matrix multiplies.

01:14:28.050 --> 01:14:30.025
So torch.compile,
as I said before,

01:14:30.025 --> 01:14:32.400
can do things like if it knows
the shape of the matrices,

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:34.360
can figure out which
kernels to dispatch.

01:14:34.360 --> 01:14:36.190
It is very good at those things.

01:14:36.190 --> 01:14:38.860
I doubt that you can get
much better than that.

01:14:38.860 --> 01:14:43.740
But there are things like if you
see FlashAttention 1, 2, and 3,

01:14:43.740 --> 01:14:45.750
those are pretty
non-trivial optimizations.

01:14:45.750 --> 01:14:50.340
Like these days torch.compile
and Jax's XLA compiler

01:14:50.340 --> 01:14:51.913
can do those, but
that's because we

01:14:51.913 --> 01:14:54.330
know, in hindsight, that those
are the right optimizations

01:14:54.330 --> 01:14:55.435
to do.

01:14:55.435 --> 01:14:57.810
I think some of those things
are a little bit non-trivial

01:14:57.810 --> 01:14:58.660
to figure out.

01:14:58.660 --> 01:15:01.320
FlashAttention3 has
additional hardware level

01:15:01.320 --> 01:15:04.900
optimizations that
leverage the H100 hardware.

01:15:04.900 --> 01:15:08.292
That's not obvious to
do with a JIT compiler.

01:15:08.292 --> 01:15:09.750
And so there are
some things that I

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:12.555
think are quite hard
with torch.compile

01:15:12.555 --> 01:15:13.930
that I think you
could do better.

01:15:13.930 --> 01:15:15.900
But in general, I
think the point here

01:15:15.900 --> 01:15:20.088
is you shouldn't go home and
say, I'm going to CUDA kernel--

01:15:20.088 --> 01:15:22.380
I'm going to write CUDA
kernels for every single pardon

01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:23.500
my language model.

01:15:23.500 --> 01:15:25.663
That's probably not a
good use of your time.

01:15:25.663 --> 01:15:27.330
But if you're writing
a new architecture

01:15:27.330 --> 01:15:30.957
with some complicated piece and
you're not getting utilization

01:15:30.957 --> 01:15:32.790
but you think you can,
that's maybe the time

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:35.160
to really bust out the Triton.

01:15:35.160 --> 01:15:40.860
OK, so we're basically at
time, but we can quickly

01:15:40.860 --> 01:15:43.840
go through one last
example of Triton.

01:15:43.840 --> 01:15:45.630
Maybe this will
be useful for you

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:48.490
in assignment two
of doing softmax.

01:15:48.490 --> 01:15:50.580
So one difference
is, until now, we

01:15:50.580 --> 01:15:52.900
were doing just basic
element wise operations.

01:15:52.900 --> 01:15:56.020
And that's really easy, because
you just operate on each element

01:15:56.020 --> 01:15:59.800
and there's no complexity
to those kinds of things.

01:15:59.800 --> 01:16:03.750
So now, let's do softmax, which
has a reduction operation where

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:06.210
you have to add across
all of the elements.

01:16:06.210 --> 01:16:07.480
So how do we do that?

01:16:07.480 --> 01:16:10.830
Well, what we want to do
is we want to normalize

01:16:10.830 --> 01:16:12.940
across each row of the matrix.

01:16:12.940 --> 01:16:16.720
And what we would like to do
is we'd like to make this fast.

01:16:16.720 --> 01:16:21.040
So a naive version of this
is going to be pretty slow.

01:16:21.040 --> 01:16:23.880
And now we're going to
write the Triton kernel.

01:16:23.880 --> 01:16:27.925
So if I wanted to be lazy, the
easiest way to do this is--

01:16:27.925 --> 01:16:30.550
OK, actually, you can think for
a moment about what the easiest

01:16:30.550 --> 01:16:31.220
way to do this.

01:16:31.220 --> 01:16:33.012
Now, let's say you want
to write a softmax.

01:16:33.012 --> 01:16:35.540
So you're going to normalize
each row of a matrix.

01:16:35.540 --> 01:16:38.090
And imagine these
matrices are pretty small.

01:16:38.090 --> 01:16:40.940
So you're just writing a
kernel for small matrices.

01:16:40.940 --> 01:16:44.780
So if you're doing this, what's
the right kind of block design?

01:16:44.780 --> 01:16:48.100
Well, maybe what we
should do is our grid

01:16:48.100 --> 01:16:49.970
should actually just be rows.

01:16:49.970 --> 01:16:52.755
So each SM is going to
handle a single row.

01:16:52.755 --> 01:16:54.380
That's kind of the
optimal thing to do.

01:16:54.380 --> 01:16:56.540
Because if we can fit
a whole row into an SM,

01:16:56.540 --> 01:17:00.530
then we just sum across that row
in the SM and then we divide.

01:17:00.530 --> 01:17:01.760
That's great.

01:17:01.760 --> 01:17:05.110
And so that's going to be the
simple design for our very

01:17:05.110 --> 01:17:06.950
naive softmax kernel here.

01:17:06.950 --> 01:17:09.250
So all we're going to do
is that we're going to make

01:17:09.250 --> 01:17:12.510
the blocksize, basically--

01:17:12.510 --> 01:17:13.010
sorry.

01:17:13.010 --> 01:17:15.020
We're going to make
each block a row,

01:17:15.020 --> 01:17:17.440
and so the blocksize
should be number of columns

01:17:17.440 --> 01:17:21.050
plus a little bit of buffer to
be able to fit all the columns.

01:17:21.050 --> 01:17:23.420
So this is Triton
next power of x2 of n,

01:17:23.420 --> 01:17:27.010
and that's a nice way of
padding out your columns.

01:17:27.010 --> 01:17:29.950
And then, I'm going to
make each block a row.

01:17:29.950 --> 01:17:32.950
So the number of blocks is
exactly the number of rows.

01:17:32.950 --> 01:17:36.490
And then I have my
Triton softmax kernel,

01:17:36.490 --> 01:17:38.620
which is written in the
way that you expect.

01:17:38.620 --> 01:17:42.490
So now we have a matrix
rather than a vector.

01:17:42.490 --> 01:17:44.560
So we have x pointers,
we have y pointers,

01:17:44.560 --> 01:17:47.280
we need the strides
of the matrices,

01:17:47.280 --> 01:17:50.580
and then we can basically figure
out what row index I'm in.

01:17:50.580 --> 01:17:52.240
I can get the column offsets.

01:17:52.240 --> 01:17:55.060
This is going to be the
same kind of code as before.

01:17:55.060 --> 01:17:56.820
In fact, getting the
row offset is simpler

01:17:56.820 --> 01:17:59.580
because each row is a block.

01:17:59.580 --> 01:18:01.710
And then now, I'm
going to do basically

01:18:01.710 --> 01:18:02.710
the same kind of stuff.

01:18:02.710 --> 01:18:07.240
I'm going to load in each row
into my SM's local memory,

01:18:07.240 --> 01:18:09.442
and then I'm going to
do computation exactly

01:18:09.442 --> 01:18:10.900
in a way that looks
like a softmax.

01:18:10.900 --> 01:18:13.900
I have my row, I subtract
my max, I take the exponent,

01:18:13.900 --> 01:18:15.840
I sum it, and then
I divide, which

01:18:15.840 --> 01:18:18.730
is going to give me my
softmax normalized row,

01:18:18.730 --> 01:18:20.920
and I write it back
to global memory.

01:18:20.920 --> 01:18:22.890
No complexity at all.

01:18:22.890 --> 01:18:26.160
Whenever your computations
fit nicely in an SM,

01:18:26.160 --> 01:18:28.790
writing Triton code looks
very similar to writing

01:18:28.790 --> 01:18:31.820
just normal Python code, just
with a little bit of load

01:18:31.820 --> 01:18:35.180
and store and keeping track
of where the blocks are.

01:18:35.180 --> 01:18:38.450
So life is pretty
simple, let's go back--

01:18:38.450 --> 01:18:39.470
oh wait, where were we?

01:18:39.470 --> 01:18:40.850
To the Triton.

01:18:40.850 --> 01:18:41.520
Here we go.

01:18:41.520 --> 01:18:45.310
And then we can see how fast all
of our different pieces of code

01:18:45.310 --> 01:18:45.810
are.

01:18:45.810 --> 01:18:47.760
So I'll zoom out again
just to make sure.

01:18:47.760 --> 01:18:50.810
OK, so manual time
takes 3.7 seconds.

01:18:50.810 --> 01:18:54.840
Our compile time is 1.3
seconds for torch.compile,

01:18:54.840 --> 01:18:58.280
and the PyTorch
time is 1.5 seconds,

01:18:58.280 --> 01:19:01.350
and the Triton time
is 1.9 seconds,

01:19:01.350 --> 01:19:04.220
so it's still a little bit slow.

01:19:04.220 --> 01:19:07.850
torch.compile can actually do
better than the native PyTorch

01:19:07.850 --> 01:19:09.770
implementation,
especially when it

01:19:09.770 --> 01:19:13.670
knows about the shapes and
sizes of certain operations.

01:19:13.670 --> 01:19:16.350
So finally, we can
look in the profiler.

01:19:16.350 --> 01:19:19.170
The manual softmax is
kind of a disaster here.

01:19:19.170 --> 01:19:21.860
You see all sorts of
crazy operations happening

01:19:21.860 --> 01:19:23.120
all over the place.

01:19:23.120 --> 01:19:24.640
Let me clear this.

01:19:24.640 --> 01:19:25.820
If we go back up here.

01:19:25.820 --> 01:19:26.710
OK, Yep.

01:19:26.710 --> 01:19:28.780
We see all sorts of
operations happening.

01:19:28.780 --> 01:19:30.670
We have x, we have
max, we have sum,

01:19:30.670 --> 01:19:32.380
because we've implemented
things naively,

01:19:32.380 --> 01:19:34.570
and we've got memory reads
and writes everywhere.

01:19:34.570 --> 01:19:38.950
The compiled softmax is just
going to be one fused softmax

01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:41.470
operation that goes quite fast.

01:19:41.470 --> 01:19:43.420
And then we've got
PyTorch softmax,

01:19:43.420 --> 01:19:48.520
which is also one CUDA
kernel call, and same thing

01:19:48.520 --> 01:19:50.900
with our Triton softmax.

01:19:50.900 --> 01:19:52.960
We have our nice
Triton softmax kernel

01:19:52.960 --> 01:19:56.380
that is a single fused
kernel for everything.

01:19:56.380 --> 01:19:59.180
OK, I won't go through
the PTX code for this.

01:19:59.180 --> 01:20:01.870
I think we're kind
of at time, and I

01:20:01.870 --> 01:20:04.180
don't want to drag you
through that low level again,

01:20:04.180 --> 01:20:08.110
but hopefully this has given
you a flavor of lower level GPU

01:20:08.110 --> 01:20:12.440
programming for the purpose of
making language models go fast,

01:20:12.440 --> 01:20:15.610
and hopefully you'll have
fun doing assignment two.

01:20:15.610 --> 01:20:17.160
Thanks.
